var QBANK_BIOLOGY = {
WBI11:[
{id:"BIO-WBI11-01",topics:["T1A"],ref:"WBI11 • June 2019 • Q1 style",title:"Food tests & sugars",marks:7,diff:1,time:"7 min",
q:"A student tests three solutions: glucose, sucrose, starch.\n(a) Describe Benedict's test and give the result for EACH solution. (4)\n(b) Describe how to test for the non-reducing sugar, with the result for sucrose. (3)",
hint:"Direct Benedict's only catches reducing sugars. Hydrolyse the rest.",
ans:["(a) Add Benedict's solution, heat to ~80 °C in a water bath. Glucose: brick-red precipitate (reducing). Sucrose: stays blue. Starch: stays blue.","(b) Boil the sample with dilute HCl (hydrolysis of the glycosidic bond), cool, neutralise with NaHCO₃/NaOH, then re-test with Benedict's and heat → sucrose now gives a brick-red precipitate (glucose + fructose released)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-02",topics:["T1A"],ref:"WBI11 • Jan 2022 • Q2 style",title:"Condensation & hydrolysis of polymers",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) State the type of bond formed when two amino acids join, and name the reaction. (2)\n(b) Explain why a protein made of 32 amino acids requires a minimum of 96 bases in a gene. (2)\n(c) Name TWO structural features that differ between a starch molecule and a cellulose molecule, and link one to a function. (3)",
hint:"1 aa = 3 bases (codon). β-linkages → straight chains → microfibrils.",
ans:["(a) A PEPTIDE bond, formed by a CONDENSATION reaction (water released).","(b) Each amino acid is coded by a TRIPLET (3 bases) → 32 × 3 = 96 bases minimum (before introns/stop codons).","(c) Starch: α-glucose, coiled/branched (energy store, compact, soluble). Cellulose: β-glucose, straight unbranched chains that H-bond into microfibrils → strong, provides rigidity/support in the cell wall."]},

{id:"BIO-WBI11-03",topics:["T1A"],ref:"WBI11 • Jan 2020 • Q2 style",title:"Water & its properties",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Explain how TWO properties of water make it suitable as a transport medium in blood. (4)\n(b) Explain why large organisms need a mass-transport system. (2)",
hint:"Solvent + high SHC + cohesion. SA:V falls with size.",
ans:["(a) Excellent solvent (polar) → carries ions, glucose, urea dissolved. High specific heat capacity → blood temperature stays stable despite heat transport. (Cohesion → continuous columns; incompressible → pressure transmission.)","(b) Small SA:V → diffusion too slow over large distances; high metabolic demand needs fast bulk delivery of O₂/glucose and removal of CO₂/wastes."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-04",topics:["T1B"],ref:"WBI11 • June 2021 • Q3 style",title:"Capillaries, tissue fluid & lymph",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Explain how tissue fluid is formed at the arteriole end of a capillary. (4)\n(b) Explain why fluid is reabsorbed at the venule end, and what happens to fluid that is not reabsorbed. (3)",
hint:"Hydrostatic pressure out; osmosis + low pressure back; lymph drains excess.",
ans:["(a) High hydrostatic (blood) pressure forces small molecules (water, ions, glucose, O₂) through capillary pores; cells and plasma proteins are too large to leave.","(b) Pressure has fallen (friction along the capillary) and the water potential is lower in the blood (proteins remain) → osmosis draws most fluid back. The excess (~10%) drains into lymph vessels → returned via the lymphatic system. Blockage → oedema."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-05",topics:["T1B"],ref:"WBI11 • June 2023 • Q3 style",title:"Magnification & SA:V (hydra)",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"A hydra has a body length of 1.8 mm and a diameter of 1 mm.\n(a) A drawing of it is 4.9 cm long. Calculate the magnification. (2)\n(b) Using SA = 2πrl, calculate its surface area in mm². (2)\n(c) Explain why single-celled organisms can rely on diffusion for gas exchange but large organisms cannot. (4)",
hint:"M = I ÷ A (convert units!). r = 0.5 mm.",
ans:["(a) Image = 4.9 cm = 49 mm; actual = 1.8 mm. M = 49 ÷ 1.8 = 27.2 ≈ 2.7 × 10¹.","(b) r = 0.5 mm, l = 1.8 mm → SA = 2π × 0.5 × 1.8 = 1.8π ≈ 5.7 mm².","(c) As size increases, volume (∝ L³) grows faster than surface area (∝ L²) → SA:V falls → diffusion across the membrane can no longer supply the metabolic needs of the interior. The hydra's small SA:V gives a short diffusion path and large relative surface → diffusion suffices; large organisms need a transport system and flattened/branched exchange surfaces."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-06",topics:["T1C"],ref:"WBI11 • Jan 2023 • Q1 style",title:"Haemoglobin & the Bohr effect",marks:9,diff:3,time:"11 min",
q:"(a) Explain the sigmoid shape of the haemoglobin–oxygen dissociation curve. (4)\n(b) Explain the advantage of the Bohr effect to actively respiring muscle. (3)\n(c) Suggest why myoglobin's curve lies to the left of haemoglobin's. (2)",
hint:"Cooperative binding. CO₂ → H⁺ → shift right. Myoglobin stores.",
ans:["(a) Cooperative (allosteric) binding: the first O₂ is hard to load (low affinity at low pO₂, shallow start); each O₂ binding changes haemoglobin's shape, easing the next (steep middle); the final sites saturate (plateau as all four are loaded).","(b) Active muscle produces CO₂ (→ H⁺, lower pH) → curve shifts right → haemoglobin's affinity falls → MORE O₂ is unloaded exactly where it is needed.","(c) Myoglobin has a higher affinity → it loads O₂ where haemoglobin releases it and only unloads at very low pO₂ — an O₂ store/reserve for muscle."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-07",topics:["T1C"],ref:"WBI11 • June 2023 • Q2 style",title:"The cardiac cycle (P–V graph)",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"A pressure–volume trace of the left ventricle shows one complete cycle lasting 0.7 s, with the AV valves closing at the start of ventricular systole.\n(a) Describe the sequence of events from atrial systole to the end of ventricular systole. (4)\n(b) Explain why the left ventricular wall is thicker than the right's. (2)\n(c) If the ventricle is full and no ejection is possible, suggest what happens to ventricular pressure and to the valves. (2)",
hint:"Pressure opens/closes valves. Left pumps to the whole body.",
ans:["(a) Atrial systole: atria contract, pressure rises above ventricles → AV valves open → blood pushed into ventricles. Ventricular systole: ventricles contract, pressure rises sharply → AV valves shut (lub; prevents backflow) → when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure the semilunar valves open → blood ejected into the aorta. Then relaxation: pressure falls, semilunar valves shut (dub), filling resumes.","(b) The left ventricle pumps around the WHOLE systemic circuit (long, high-resistance) → needs much higher pressure → more cardiac muscle; the right only pumps to the nearby, low-resistance pulmonary circuit.","(c) Pressure would rise steeply (closed pump with outflow blocked) until it forced the semilunar valve open or damaged the muscle; the AV valves remain shut (higher ventricular pressure keeps them closed)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-08",topics:["T1C"],ref:"WBI11 • Oct 2021 • Q3 style",title:"Atheroma & smoking",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"(a) Describe how an atheroma forms in a coronary artery. (4)\n(b) Explain how smoking increases the risk of cardiovascular disease. (4)",
hint:"Damage → LDL → plaque. Nicotine, CO, chemicals — three weapons.",
ans:["(a) Endothelium damaged (high BP, smoking, age) → LDL cholesterol accumulates underneath → white blood cells engulf it (foam cells) → a fibrous plaque/cap forms → the lumen narrows and the wall stiffens (atherosclerosis).","(b) Nicotine: addictive, raises heart rate and BP (more mechanical damage). Carbon monoxide: binds haemoglobin → less O₂ delivered → heart works harder. Tar/chemicals: damage the endothelium directly and promote clotting. Together they accelerate atheroma formation and the risk of a coronary clot (myocardial infarction)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI11-09",topics:["T1C"],ref:"WBI11 • June 2022 • Q5 style",title:"Stents & blood-clotting risk",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Explain how a stent treats a narrowed coronary artery. (3)\n(b) Patients given a stent take anticoagulant drugs. Explain why these are needed, and state ONE risk of the treatment. (3)",
hint:"Metal mesh holds the plaque back. Platelets → thromboplastin → prothrombin → thrombin.",
ans:["(a) A balloon-mounted metal mesh (stent) is expanded inside the narrowed artery → it compresses the plaque against the wall and holds the lumen open (maintaining blood flow to the heart muscle).","(b) The stent (a foreign surface) triggers the clotting cascade — platelets release thromboplastin → prothrombin is converted to thrombin → fibrinogen → fibrin → a clot could form on the stent and re-block the artery; anticoagulants/antiplatelets prevent this. Risk: excessive bleeding/bruising (or internal haemorrhage) because clots elsewhere are also inhibited."]},
],
WBI12:[
{id:"BIO-WBI12-01",topics:["T2A"],ref:"WBI12 • June 2020 • Q1 style",title:"Membrane structure & function",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Describe the structure of the phospholipid bilayer. (3)\n(b) State TWO different functions of membrane proteins. (2)\n(c) Explain why cholesterol in the membrane helps maintain membrane integrity at both high and low temperatures. (2)",
hint:"Hydrophilic heads out, hydrophobic tails in. Proteins do the jobs.",
ans:["(a) Phospholipids have a hydrophilic (polar) head and two hydrophobic fatty-acid tails; they arrange in two layers with heads facing the aqueous environments (inside/outside) and tails facing each other in the middle, forming a stable barrier to water-soluble substances.","(b) Any two: channels (facilitated diffusion of ions/water), carriers (facilitated/active transport), receptors (hormone/neurotransmitter binding), enzymes, glycoproteins (cell recognition), protein channels. (One mark each, function must be specific.)","(c) At high temperatures cholesterol restricts phospholipid movement (prevents the membrane becoming too fluid); at low temperatures it prevents the fatty-acid tails from packing tightly (prevents it becoming too rigid/crystallising)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-02",topics:["T2A"],ref:"WBI12 • Jan 2023 • Q2 style",title:"Types of membrane transport",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"A cell takes up: (i) oxygen, (ii) glucose at high external concentration, (iii) potassium ions against a concentration gradient.\nFor EACH, name the type of transport and explain your choice. (6)",
hint:"Three different mechanisms — match each to the evidence in the scenario.",
ans:["(i) Simple diffusion — O₂ is small and non-polar, so it moves down its concentration gradient straight through the phospholipid bilayer; no protein and no energy are required.","(ii) Facilitated diffusion — glucose is polar and too large to cross the bilayer, so it moves down its gradient through a specific carrier protein (e.g. a GLUT transporter); no energy is required.","(iii) Active transport — the K⁺ is moved against its concentration gradient, which requires a carrier protein and energy from ATP hydrolysis (cf. the Na⁺/K⁺ pump)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-03",topics:["T2A"],ref:"WBI12 • Oct 2022 • Q3 style",title:"Bulk transport & exocytosis",marks:4,diff:2,time:"5 min",
q:"(a) A white blood cell engulfs a bacterium. Name this process and state its energy requirement. (2)\n(b) A pancreatic cell releases digestive enzymes. Name the process involved and one reason a cell uses it. (2)",
hint:"In = endocytosis; out = exocytosis. Both need ATP and vesicles.",
ans:["(a) Phagocytosis (a form of endocytosis / bulk transport) — the membrane engulfs the particle into a vesicle; it requires energy (ATP) because the membrane must be remodelled.","(b) Exocytosis — the vesicle fuses with the cell membrane and releases its contents outside; cells use it because enzymes (large, polar molecules) cannot cross the membrane by diffusion and must be secreted in bulk."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-04",topics:["T2A"],ref:"WBI12 • June 2021 • Q4 style",title:"Osmosis — potato cylinders",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"Potato cylinders (initial mass 2.0 g each) were placed in sucrose solutions of 0.0–1.0 mol/dm³ for 30 min. In 0.6 mol/dm³ the mass fell to 1.5 g.\n(a) Explain the change in mass in terms of water movement. (3)\n(b) The mass change was zero at 0.35 mol/dm³. Deduce the water potential of the potato cells relative to that solution. (2)\n(c) Suggest why the experiment should use at least five cylinders per concentration. (1)",
hint:"Water leaves toward the higher solute concentration; zero change = equilibrium.",
ans:["(a) The 0.6 mol/dm³ solution has a lower water potential than the cell sap → water leaves the cells by osmosis (down the water-potential gradient) → the cylinders lose mass.","(b) No net water movement means the potato cell sap and the solution have the SAME water potential (they are in osmotic equilibrium).","(c) To allow for random variation/individual differences → a mean can be calculated and anomalies identified (reliability)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-05",topics:["T2B"],ref:"WBI12 • Jan 2024 • Q1 style",title:"Protein structure & denaturation",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"(a) State the FOUR levels of protein structure, giving one feature of each. (4)\n(b) Explain how a rise in temperature above ~60 °C affects an enzyme's activity. (4)",
hint:"Primary→quaternary. Heat breaks weak bonds → active-site shape lost.",
ans:["(a) Primary — the linear sequence of amino acids (held by peptide bonds). Secondary — local coiling/folding (α-helix/β-pleated sheet) held by hydrogen bonds between the backbone. Tertiary — the overall 3D globular shape held by R-group interactions (H-bonds, ionic, disulphide bridges, hydrophobic). Quaternary — arrangement of two or more polypeptide subunits (e.g. haemoglobin's four subunits).","(b) High temperature adds kinetic energy → the weak bonds (H-bonds, ionic, hydrophobic) holding the tertiary structure vibrate apart → the protein DENATURES → the active site changes shape → the substrate no longer fits (lock-and-key/induced fit fails) → enzyme–substrate complexes fall → activity rate falls to zero (irreversibly, if severe)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-06",topics:["T2B"],ref:"WBI12 • June 2022 • Q3 style",title:"DNA structure & base-pair calculations",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"A DNA molecule contains 30 000 base pairs, of which 12 000 bases are adenine.\n(a) How many thymine bases does it contain? (1)\n(b) How many guanine bases? (2)\n(c) State the TWO structural features of the DNA double helix that allow accurate replication. (2)\n(d) Explain why the sequence of bases in a gene determines the amino-acid sequence of a protein. (2)",
hint:"A=T, G+C make up the rest. Complementary pairing + code is triplets.",
ans:["(a) 12 000 (adenine pairs only with thymine, 1:1).","(b) Total bases = 60 000. A + T = 24 000, so G + C = 36 000 → guanine = 18 000 (G=C).","(c) Complementary base pairing (A–T, G–C) means each strand can serve as a template for an identical new strand; the two antiparallel strands are held by hydrogen bonds that can be temporarily broken (unzipped by helicase).","(d) The code is read in TRIPLETS (codons), each of which specifies a particular amino acid; so the base sequence of the gene (via its mRNA codons) dictates the order of amino acids joined in the polypeptide."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-07",topics:["T2C"],ref:"WBI12 • Jan 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Transcription & translation",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"A gene has the template (3′→5′) sequence: TAC GGT AAA CTA.\n(a) Write the mRNA codons produced by transcription. (2)\n(b) Using the codon table — GGU = glycine, UUU = phenylalanine, GAA = glutamic acid, GAU = aspartic acid — give the amino-acid sequence of the peptide. (2)\n(c) State WHERE transcription and translation each occur in a eukaryotic cell. (2)\n(d) Name the molecule that carries amino acids to the ribosome and state how it matches the mRNA codon. (2)",
hint:"mRNA is complementary to the template (U for T); tRNA anticodons pair with codons.",
ans:["(a) mRNA (5′→3′): AUG CCA UUU GAU.","(b) AUG = start (methionine); CCA = proline… using the given table: UUU → phenylalanine, GAU → aspartic acid, GGU (if present) → glycine; the peptide is Met–(CCA, not in table – name from your table)–Phe–Asp. Award marks for the three table look-ups: Phe (UUU), Asp (GAU), and the start codon AUG → methionine.","(c) Transcription in the NUCLEUS (DNA → mRNA, then mRNA processed and exported through nuclear pores); translation in the CYTOPLASM on ribosomes (free or bound to rough ER).","(d) tRNA (transfer RNA) — each tRNA has an anticodon that base-pairs with a complementary mRNA codon at the ribosome, and it carries the corresponding amino acid."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-08",topics:["T2C"],ref:"WBI12 • Oct 2021 • Q5 style",title:"Mutations & their effects",marks:7,diff:3,time:"8 min",
q:"A codon normally reads GAA (glutamic acid).\n(a) A single base is changed so the codon becomes UAA. Name this type of mutation and explain its effect on the protein. (3)\n(b) A base is deleted further along the same gene. Explain how the result differs from the change in (a). (2)\n(c) State why many mutations have no effect on the organism. (2)",
hint:"UAA is a stop signal. Deletions shift the reading frame. The code is degenerate.",
ans:["(a) A point (base-substitution) mutation creating a NONSENSE (stop) codon — translation terminates early → a truncated (shorter) polypeptide, which is usually non-functional.","(b) A base deletion shifts the READING FRAME — every triplet after the deletion is read differently, so the entire downstream amino-acid sequence changes (and a stop codon may appear early or late), not just one residue as in (a).","(c) The genetic code is DEGENERATE (several codons code for the same amino acid) — so a base change can still specify the same amino acid (silent/synonymous mutation); also many genes are non-coding (introns/intergenic) or the changed amino acid is not in a critical region (e.g. active site)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-09",topics:["T2C"],ref:"WBI12 • June 2023 • Q6 style",title:"Monohybrid cross & chi-squared",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"In a plant, flower colour is controlled by one gene: allele R (red) is dominant to r (white).\n(a) Two red-flowered plants are crossed and 320 red and 106 white offspring appear. Deduce the genotypes of the parents. (2)\n(b) State the expected ratio and calculate the expected numbers for each phenotype. (2)\n(c) Complete the chi-squared calculation: χ² = Σ(observed − expected)²/expected. (df = 1; critical value at p = 0.05 is 3.88.) (3)\n(d) State your conclusion about the hypothesis that the parents are both Rr. (1)",
hint:"3:1 appears → both parents heterozygous. χ² < 3.88 → accept.",
ans:["(a) WHITE offspring (rr) can only come if BOTH parents carry r; both are red (R_) → both parents are Rr.","(b) Rr × Rr → expected 3 red : 1 white. Total = 426 → expected red = 319.5, white = 106.5.","(c) χ² = [(320−319.5)²/319.5] + [(106−106.5)²/106.5] = (0.25/319.5) + (0.25/106.5) ≈ 0.0008 + 0.0023 = 0.0031.","(d) χ² (0.003) is much less than the critical value 3.88 → the difference is due to chance → ACCEPT the hypothesis that both parents are Rr (the 3:1 ratio fits)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI12-10",topics:["T2C"],ref:"WBI12 • Jan 2024 • Q2 style",title:"Pedigree — autosomal recessive (CF style)",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele c (genotypes: CC or Cc = healthy, cc = affected).\nIn a family, two healthy parents have an affected son.\n(a) State the genotypes of the parents and explain how you know. (3)\n(b) If they have another child, calculate the probability it is affected. (2)\n(c) State why the condition is described as autosomal rather than sex-linked. (1)",
hint:"Two healthy parents + affected child → both carriers. 1/4 each pregnancy.",
ans:["(a) The affected son is cc — one c from EACH parent; both parents are healthy (not cc) → both must be Cc (carriers/heterozygous).","(b) Cc × Cc → 1 CC : 2 Cc : 1 cc → probability of an affected (cc) child = 1/4 (25%), independently of previous children.","(c) It affects a male and was inherited from both a mother and a father in the same way as any autosomal gene — the allele is on a non-sex (autosome) chromosome, not the X (if X-linked recessive, a mother could pass it to a son without herself being a carrier in the same pattern, and the father's genotype would be the determining factor)."]},
],
WBI21:[
{id:"BIO-WBI21-01",topics:["T3A"],ref:"WBI21 • June 2022 • Q1 style",title:"Microscopy & magnification",marks:5,diff:1,time:"6 min",
q:"An electron micrograph of a mitochondrion is magnified ×120 000. A cristae is measured as 1.5 µm on the image.\n(a) Calculate its actual length in nm. (3)\n(b) State ONE advantage and ONE limitation of a transmission electron microscope compared with a light microscope. (2)",
hint:"Actual = image ÷ magnification. EM = dead, fixed samples.",
ans:["(a) Actual = 1.5 µm ÷ 120 000 = 1.25 × 10⁻⁵ µm = 0.0125 µm = 12.5 nm.","(b) Advantage: much higher resolution (can see membranes/ribosomes ~0.2 nm vs ~200 nm) and higher magnification. Limitation: samples must be dead, fixed, dehydrated and viewed in a vacuum (no living processes, thin sections only)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-02",topics:["T3A"],ref:"WBI21 • Jan 2023 • Q2 style",title:"Organelles & the secretory pathway",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"(a) State the function of EACH: rough ER, Golgi apparatus, lysosome, mitochondrion. (4)\n(b) A gland cell secretes a digestive protein. Put the following in the correct order: Golgi vesicle, rough ER, nuclear pore, plasma membrane, ribosome, mRNA. (4)",
hint:"Nucleus → rER → vesicle → Golgi → vesicle → membrane. Then the organelle jobs.",
ans:["(a) Rough ER — site of synthesis and initial modification of secretory/membrane proteins (ribosomes attached). Golgi — modifies (e.g. glycosylation), sorts and packages proteins into vesicles for secretion/lysosomes. Lysosome — contains hydrolytic enzymes for intracellular digestion (worn organelles, ingested material). Mitochondrion — aerobic respiration producing ATP (the cell's main energy supplier).","(b) mRNA → nuclear pore → ribosome (on rough ER) → rough ER → Golgi vesicle → Golgi → (vesicle to) plasma membrane. Award the sequence: genetic message leaves nucleus via a pore, is translated at a ribosome on the rough ER, the protein is processed in the ER, travels to the Golgi in a vesicle, is packaged there, and a vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane to release it."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-03",topics:["T3A"],ref:"WBI21 • June 2023 • Q3 style",title:"Prokaryotes vs eukaryotes",marks:7,diff:1,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) List THREE structural features that distinguish a bacterial cell from an animal cell. (3)\n(b) Explain why bacteria can be made to produce human proteins (e.g. insulin) using recombinant DNA technology. (2)\n(c) State why antibiotics that target 70S ribosomes do not harm human cells. (2)",
hint:"No nucleus/organelles/70S ribosomes. Universal code. Human ribosomes are 80S.",
ans:["(a) Any three: no nucleus (circular DNA in a nucleoid region); no membrane-bound organelles (no mitochondria/ER/Golgi); smaller 70S ribosomes; often a cell wall (peptidoglycan) and/or plasmids; much smaller (~1–5 µm vs ~10–50 µm).","(b) The genetic code is UNIVERSAL — the same triplets code for the same amino acids in all organisms, so a human gene inserted into a bacterium is transcribed and translated into the correct human protein.","(c) Human (eukaryotic) cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S, so drugs that specifically inhibit the bacterial 70S ribosome (e.g. the 30S/50S subunits) do not bind human ribosomes — selective toxicity. (Human mitochondrial ribosomes are 70S-like, which is why some side-effects occur.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-04",topics:["T3A"],ref:"WBI21 • Oct 2022 • Q4 style",title:"SA:V & diffusion into agar",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"Cubes of agar (a model for a cell) with sides 1 cm, 2 cm and 4 cm are immersed in phenolphthalein indicator. After 30 min the colour has diffused 1 cm in from every surface.\n(a) Calculate the SA:V ratio for each cube. (3)\n(b) Which cube is completely de-colourised? Explain. (1)\n(c) Explain how this model shows why cells are small. (2)",
hint:"Cube SA:V = 6/L. 1 cm diffusion depth.",
ans:["(a) 1 cm cube: SA = 6 cm², V = 1 cm³ → 6:1. 2 cm cube: SA = 24 cm², V = 8 cm³ → 3:1. 4 cm cube: SA = 96 cm², V = 64 cm³ → 1.5:1.","(b) The 1 cm cube — the indicator (diffused 1 cm in from all faces) reaches the centre (half the side = 0.5 cm < 1 cm); the 2 cm cube's centre is exactly at the limit, the 4 cm cube retains a coloured core.","(c) As size increases, SA:V falls, so the relative surface available for diffusion shrinks compared with the volume that must be supplied; the 4 cm cube has an un-served core, just as a large cell's centre would starve of nutrients/O₂ — so cells divide (or evolve shapes) to keep SA:V high."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-05",topics:["T3B"],ref:"WBI21 • June 2021 • Q2 style",title:"Mitosis vs meiosis",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"(a) State the purpose of mitosis and of meiosis. (2)\n(b) State the number and genetic nature of the cells produced by each, starting from a diploid cell with 2n = 4. (4)\n(c) State the TWO processes in meiosis that generate genetic variation, and briefly explain each. (2)",
hint:"Growth/repair vs gametes. 2 identical vs 4 different. Assortment + crossing over.",
ans:["(a) Mitosis — growth, repair and replacement of body (somatic) cells; meiosis — production of haploid gametes (and spores) for sexual reproduction.","(b) Mitosis: ONE division → 2 daughter cells, each DIPLOID (2n = 4), genetically IDENTICAL to the parent. Meiosis: TWO divisions → 4 cells, each HAPLOID (n = 2), genetically DIFFERENT from the parent and from each other.","(c) Independent assortment — at metaphase I each homologous pair lines up randomly, so which parental chromosome goes to each pole is a 50:50 event per pair (2ⁿ combinations). Crossing over — in prophase I, non-sister chromatids of homologues exchange matching segments, creating new allele combinations on a chromosome."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-06",topics:["T3B"],ref:"WBI21 • Jan 2024 • Q3 style",title:"Chromosomes & chromatids through division",marks:7,diff:3,time:"8 min",
q:"A cell in G2 of the cell cycle (before mitosis) has 6 chromosomes.\n(a) How many chromatids are present? (1)\n(b) During anaphase of mitosis, how many chromosomes are moving to EACH pole? (1)\n(c) If the same starting cell instead underwent meiosis, state the chromosome number in EACH of the four final cells, and in which division the halving occurs. (2)\n(d) Explain why DNA replication happens only ONCE before meiosis's two divisions. (3)",
hint:"G2 = replicated (2 chromatids each). Halving = meiosis I.",
ans:["(a) 12 (each of the 6 chromosomes has replicated into two sister chromatids).","(b) 6 (the sister chromatids separate at anaphase; each chromatid is now counted as one chromosome — 6 per pole, so each daughter cell ends with 6).","(c) Each final cell has 3 chromosomes (n = 3); the halving occurs in MEIOSIS I (homologues separate), while meiosis II only splits sister chromatids (like mitosis).","(d) Because meiosis is one replication + two divisions: the chromosome number must be halved overall (2n → n); replicating twice would leave the gametes with the wrong number (and the homologous pairs needed for crossing over/pairing in prophase I would not exist). One copy per homologue is enough for each of the two divisions."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-07",topics:["T3B"],ref:"WBI21 • June 2022 • Q4 style",title:"Gamete structure & function",marks:6,diff:1,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Describe TWO structural adaptations of a human sperm cell, linking each to its function. (4)\n(b) State the main structural difference between a human ovum and a sperm cell, and explain its purpose. (2)",
hint:"Flagellum, mitochondrial-rich midpiece, acrosome. Ovum = food store.",
ans:["(a) Any two, each linked: flagellum (tail) — for movement/swimming toward the ovum; midpiece packed with mitochondria — respiration providing the ATP to power the flagellum; acrosome (enzyme vesicle) — releases enzymes to digest through the ovum's outer layers (zona pellucida); small cytoplasm — minimises drag, maximises speed; haploid nucleus — so fusion restores the diploid number.","(b) The ovum has a large cytoplasm containing nutrient (yolk) reserves — to nourish the early embryo during the first divisions until implantation (the sperm's job is only delivery, so it is streamlined instead)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-08",topics:["T3B"],ref:"WBI21 • Oct 2023 • Q5 style",title:"Plant fertilisation (double fertilisation)",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"Describe what happens from the landing of a pollen grain on a stigma to the formation of a seed in a flowering plant. (6)",
hint:"Pollen tube → two male nuclei → egg + polar cells → seed/fruit.",
ans:["Pollen grain lands on the (compatible) stigma → it germinates and grows a POLLEN TUBE down the style toward the ovule, guided by chemicals released by the ovule. The tube carries TWO male nuclei: one fuses with the EGG CELL → a diploid ZYGOTE (which develops into the embryo); the other fuses with the two POLAR CELLS → a triploid ENDOSPERM (the food store for the developing embryo) — this is DOUBLE fertilisation. The fertilised ovule develops into a SEED (embryo + endosperm + seed coat) and the ovary develops into the FRUIT (which helps disperse the seeds)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-09",topics:["T3C"],ref:"WBI21 • Jan 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Differentiation & cloning evidence",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) A sheep's udder (mammary gland) cell nucleus was transferred into an enucleated egg cell, which developed into a full sheep (Dolly).\nWhat does this experiment demonstrate about differentiation? (3)\n(b) Explain how cell differentiation produces different cell types from cells with the same genome. (3)\n(c) Give ONE environmental factor that can change the phenotype of an organism, with an example. (1)",
hint:"Same genome, different gene expression. Phenotype = genotype + environment.",
ans:["(a) It demonstrates that differentiated (specialised) ADULT cells still contain the COMPLETE genome — all the genes needed to make a whole organism — and that differentiation is a matter of which genes are SWITCHED ON/OFF (gene expression), not loss of genes. The nucleus retained its totipotent potential when placed in the egg cytoplasm.","(b) Almost every cell contains the same full set of genes, but in different cell types different genes are expressed: transcription factors and epigenetic marks (e.g. DNA methylation, histone modification) switch specific genes on or off, so a muscle cell makes contractile proteins while a neuron makes neurotransmitter machinery — same DNA, different RNA/protein products.","(c) E.g. environment/nutrition — a plant with the tall genotype stays dwarf if it lacks gibberellin responsiveness or adequate light/nutrients; or hydrangea flower colour (blue in acidic soil, pink in alkaline soil) — the environment determines which possibility the genotype allows."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI21-10",topics:["T3C"],ref:"WBI21 • June 2023 • Q5 style",title:"Stem cells — potency, uses & ethics",marks:8,diff:2,time:"10 min",
q:"(a) Define a stem cell and distinguish totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent. (4)\n(b) Explain how stem cells could be used to treat Type 1 diabetes, and name a source of the cells used. (2)\n(c) State ONE ethical argument for and ONE against using human embryonic stem cells. (2)",
hint:"Self-renew + differentiate. Replace the dead beta cells. Embryo status vs cures.",
ans:["(a) A stem cell is an undifferentiated cell capable of (i) self-renewal (dividing to make more stem cells) and (ii) differentiation into specialised cell types. Totipotent — can form ALL cell types including extra-embryonic tissue (placenta); only the earliest embryo cells. Pluripotent — all cell types of the body but not extra-embryonic (e.g. embryonic stem cells from the blastocyst's inner cell mass). Multipotent — a restricted range of related types (e.g. adult bone-marrow stem cells → blood-line cells only).","(b) In Type 1 diabetes the immune system destroys the insulin-producing β-cells of the pancreas; stem cells could be differentiated into replacement β-cells and transplanted to restore insulin production (e.g. from bone-marrow/adult stem cells, or pluripotent/induced-pluripotent cells; ideally the patient's own cells to avoid rejection).","(c) For — the potential to cure degenerative diseases, better drug testing (less animal use), and patient-matched cells reducing immune rejection. Against — the cells come from human EMBRYOS, which many consider to have moral status, so using them destroys them; there are also risks (tumour formation) and questions of access/cost."]},
],
WBI22:[
{id:"BIO-WBI22-01",topics:["T4A"],ref:"WBI22 • June 2022 • Q2 style",title:"Xylem vs phloem",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"Compare xylem and phloem with respect to: the type of cells, whether they are alive at maturity, what they transport, the direction of transport, and whether energy is required. (5)\nExplain why the xylem's dead, hollow, lignin-reinforced structure suits its function. (3)",
hint:"Dead vs living; up vs both ways; passive vs active. Lignin = strength + waterproof.",
ans:["Comparison: Xylem — dead at maturity, hollow tubes of lignified cells; transports water + dissolved mineral ions; direction UPWARD (roots → leaves); energy NOT required (passive transpiration pull). Phloem — living (sieve-tube elements + companion cells); transports sugars (sucrose) + amino acids (assimilates); direction BOTH ways (source → sink); energy (ATP) required (active loading/translocation).","Structure–function: the hollow, end-to-end tubes create a continuous low-resistance column for bulk water flow; the lignin reinforces the walls against the negative pressure (tension) of the transpiration pull and prevents the tubes collapsing; lignin is also waterproof, keeping the water column sealed (cohesion is maintained)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-02",topics:["T4A"],ref:"WBI22 • Jan 2024 • Q3 style",title:"Mineral deficiencies",marks:6,diff:1,time:"7 min",
q:"State the role of EACH mineral in plants and the visible deficiency symptom:\n(a) nitrate, (b) magnesium, (c) potassium. (6)",
hint:"NO₃⁻ → proteins (old leaves yellow); Mg → chlorophyll (interveinal chlorosis); K⁻ → enzymes/water potential (brown edges).",
ans:["(a) Nitrate (NO₃⁻) — absorbed and used to make amino acids → proteins/enzymes (and nucleic acids); deficiency: stunted growth and yellowing (chlorosis) of the OLDER leaves (nitrate is mobile and redistributed from old to new tissue).","(b) Magnesium (Mg²⁺) — the central ion of the chlorophyll molecule (also activates some enzymes); deficiency: interveinal CHLOROSIS (yellowing between the green veins) on older leaves, reduced photosynthesis.","(c) Potassium (K⁺) — activates enzymes, controls stomatal opening (guard-cell K⁺ pumping), and helps maintain water potential/turgor; deficiency: browning/necrosis at the edges and tips of older leaves, reduced drought tolerance."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-03",topics:["T4A"],ref:"WBI22 • Oct 2022 • Q1 style",title:"Plant vs animal cells",marks:5,diff:1,time:"6 min",
q:"State THREE structures found in a palisade mesophyll (plant) cell but not in an animal cell, and the function of each. (3)\nState TWO structures that such a plant cell would NOT contain, that are present in a typical animal cell. (2)",
hint:"Wall, chloroplasts, big vacuole. Animal-only: centrioles, lysosomes.",
ans:["Present in the plant cell only (each with function): CELL WALL (cellulose) — mechanical support/rigidity and protection, prevents over-expansion; CHLOROPLASTS (grana + stroma) — site of photosynthesis; LARGE CENTRAL VACUOLE (cell sap) — maintains turgor pressure and stores solutes. (Also: a permanent large vacuole vs many small ones.)","Absent in the plant cell (present in animals): CENTRIOLES (organise the mitotic spindle) and LYSOSOMES (intracellular digestion — the vacuole largely fulfils this role in plants)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-04",topics:["T4A"],ref:"WBI22 • June 2023 • Q6 style",title:"Developing a new drug",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"A company identifies a protein involved in a disease and wants to develop a drug that blocks it.\n(a) Outline the stages from candidate molecule to a licensed medicine, naming what is tested at each stage. (5)\n(b) Explain why most candidate molecules never reach patients. (2)",
hint:"Preclinical → Phase I/II/III → approval. Safety gates kill most.",
ans:["(a) 1. DISCOVERY — identify the target protein and find a candidate molecule that blocks it (natural product, synthetic library, or biotech). 2. PRECLINICAL — test in cells/animals for safety, dose and mechanism (most fail here). 3. CLINICAL TRIALS — Phase I (small group of healthy volunteers: safety and dose); Phase II (patients: does it work? side effects?); Phase III (large, randomised, controlled trial: confirm efficacy, detect rare side effects vs placebo). 4. REGULATORY APPROVAL (e.g. by the national medicine authority) → licensed for use, followed by Phase IV post-marketing surveillance of long-term effects in the population.","(b) At every stage there is a safety/efficacy GATE: molecules that are toxic, ineffective, poorly absorbed, or have unacceptable side effects are dropped — and Phase III requires clear benefit over existing treatments, so the vast majority fail before or during trials (only a small fraction of candidates ever reach the market)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-05",topics:["T4B"],ref:"WBI22 • Jan 2023 • Q1 style",title:"Classification & binomial names",marks:5,diff:1,time:"6 min",
q:"(a) List the taxonomic ranks between domain and species, in order. (3)\n(b) The human binomial name is Homo sapiens. State which part is the genus, and give the rules for writing a binomial name. (2)",
hint:"K-P-C-O-F-G-S. Genus capitalised, species lowercase, both italic.",
ans:["(a) Domain → KINGDOM → PHYLUM → CLASS → ORDER → FAMILY → GENUS → species (the question asks between domain and species: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus).","(b) Homo is the GENUS (sapiens the species epithet). Rules: the Genus is capitalised, the species name is lower-case, both words are written in italics (or underlined when handwritten), and organisms in the same genus share a recent common ancestor."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-06",topics:["T4B"],ref:"WBI22 • June 2021 • Q5 style",title:"Species, isolation & speciation",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"Two frog populations live on opposite sides of a mountain. Over many generations they can no longer interbreed, even when the barrier is removed.\n(a) Define a species using the biological species concept. (2)\n(b) Name the type of speciation that occurred and describe the three stages. (4)\n(c) State why the biological species concept cannot be applied to bacteria. (2)",
hint:"Interbreeding → fertile offspring. Allopatric: isolate → diverge → isolation. Asexual organisms.",
ans:["(a) A species is a group of organisms that can interbreed with each other to produce FERTILE offspring under natural conditions, and are reproductively isolated from other such groups.","(b) ALLOPATRIC speciation (geographic isolation). Stages: ① ISOLATION — the mountain (geographic barrier) splits one population into two, stopping gene flow; ② DIVERGENCE — mutation, natural selection (different environments on each side) and genetic drift cause the two populations to accumulate different alleles/genotypes over generations; ③ REPRODUCTIVE ISOLATION — enough genetic difference builds up (pre- or post-zygotic barriers, e.g. different calling times or sterile hybrids) that they can no longer interbreed even when reunited → two separate species.","(c) Bacteria reproduce ASEXUALLY (binary fission) and exchange genes horizontally (conjugation/transformation/transduction), so they do not 'interbreed to produce fertile offspring' — the concept relies on sexual reproduction and is inapplicable; bacterial 'species' are instead defined genetically (DNA sequence similarity) and morphologically."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-07",topics:["T4B"],ref:"WBI22 • Oct 2023 • Q2 style",title:"DNA barcoding & phylogenetics",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Describe how DNA barcoding could identify an unknown insect found in a forest. (3)\n(b) State TWO advantages of DNA-based classification over classification by appearance alone. (2)\n(c) State one limitation of relying on a single barcode gene. (1)",
hint:"Sequence a standard gene → match to a reference database. Objective + hidden relations; one gene ≠ the whole story.",
ans:["(a) Extract DNA from the specimen (even a small fragment) and sequence a standard 'barcode' gene region (e.g. COI for animals); compare the sequence to a reference database (e.g. BOLD) — a close match to a known species' barcode identifies it (or reveals a new species if no close match exists).","(b) Any two: it is objective/quantitative (sequence similarity, not subjective appearance); it works on fragments, larvae or cryptic morphologically identical species; it reveals true evolutionary relationships that convergent evolution can disguise; it is not affected by environmental plasticity of form.","(c) A single gene reflects only ONE locus' history — horizontal gene transfer, incomplete lineage sorting or selection on that gene can make it unrepresentative of the organism's overall ancestry, so results should be confirmed with more genes/phenotypes."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-08",topics:["T4C"],ref:"WBI22 • June 2022 • Q4 style",title:"Simpson's index calculation",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"In a 100 m² meadow a student counts: 40 daisies, 30 grasses, 20 clovers, 10 buttercups (N = 100).\n(a) Calculate Simpson's index D = 1 − [Σn(n−1) / N(N−1)]. (3)\n(b) A second meadow has the SAME four species but 97 daisies and 3 other plants combined. Calculate its D. (2)\n(c) Interpret the difference between the two meadows in terms of richness and evenness. (3)",
hint:"Show the sums. Same richness, very different evenness.",
ans:["(a) Σn(n−1) = 40×39 + 30×29 + 20×19 + 10×9 = 1560 + 870 + 380 + 90 = 2900. N(N−1) = 100×99 = 9900. D = 1 − (2900/9900) = 1 − 0.293 = 0.707.","(b) Σn(n−1) = 97×96 + 3×2 = 9312 + 6 = 9318. D = 1 − (9318/9900) = 1 − 0.941 = 0.059.","(c) Both meadows have the same RICHNESS (4 species), but meadow 1 has much higher EVENNESS (no single species dominates) → D = 0.707, a more diverse community; meadow 2 is dominated by one species (97%) → D = 0.059, much less diverse. So diversity depends on both the number of species AND how evenly individuals are shared between them."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-09",topics:["T4C"],ref:"WBI22 • Jan 2024 • Q5 style",title:"Gene pools & genetic diversity",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Define the gene pool of a population. (1)\n(b) Explain why a small, isolated population has a higher risk of extinction than a large, connected one. (4)\n(c) State how a wildlife corridor between two fragmented habitats helps a species' long-term survival. (1)",
hint:"Founder/bottleneck → low diversity → inbreeding + no adaptation. Corridors restore gene flow.",
ans:["(a) The gene pool is the complete set of alleles (all the genetic variation) present in all the individuals of a population.","(b) Small isolated populations have a small gene pool (founder/bottleneck effects) → more INBREEDING, which exposes harmful recessive alleles (inbreeding depression — reduced fertility and survival); they also have less genetic 'raw material' for natural selection, so they cannot ADAPT to environmental change (new disease, climate shift); random genetic drift can fix (or lose) alleles by chance. Together these raise extinction risk; large connected populations maintain high diversity and gene flow, buffering against all of these.","(c) The corridor allows individuals to move between the fragments, RESTORING GENE FLOW — mixing the gene pools, reducing inbreeding, and letting the species track changing conditions (increasing effective population size and adaptive capacity)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI22-10",topics:["T4C"],ref:"WBI22 • June 2023 • Q7 style",title:"Case study: orangutan conservation",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"Bornean orangutan populations have fallen by more than 80% since 1970, largely because forest is cleared for palm-oil plantations.\n(a) Explain, in terms of genetics, the consequence of the forest being split into small fragments. (3)\n(b) State ONE in-situ and ONE ex-situ conservation action for orangutans, explaining how each works. (3)\n(c) State why combining in-situ and ex-situ approaches is more effective than either alone. (1)",
hint:"Fragmentation → small populations → inbreeding. Protected forests; sanctuaries; both cover different threats.",
ans:["(a) Clearing splits the range into small, isolated fragments → each fragment holds a small sub-population with a reduced gene pool → GENE FLOW is blocked between fragments, so INBREEDING increases within each (harmful recessive alleles are expressed — inbreeding depression), and the overall adaptive capacity falls; a single local threat (disease, fire, storm) can wipe out an entire fragment, raising extinction risk.","(b) In-situ: protect/restore the remaining forest (national parks/reserves, anti-logging law, RSPO-certified sustainable palm oil, wildlife corridors) — it conserves the whole ecosystem and lets natural selection continue where the species evolved. Ex-situ: orangutan sanctuaries that rescue orphaned/injured individuals, rehabilitate them (re-teaching foraging and social skills) and re-release them — a back-up that directly adds individuals to wild populations and raises public awareness.","(c) In-situ alone is vulnerable to sudden local disasters and cannot recover animals already lost to habitat destruction; ex-situ alone cannot replace the habitat the species needs to survive in the long term — together they protect the habitat AND the gene pool (reintroducing individuals maintains numbers and genetic variation while the habitat recovers)."]},
],
WBI31:[
{id:"BIO-WBI31-01",topics:["T5A"],ref:"WBI31 • June 2022 • Q2 style",title:"The Calvin cycle",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"Describe the THREE phases of the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle), naming the key molecules and the role of ATP and NADPH. (8)",
hint:"Fixation (RuBP + CO₂ → 2 GP) → reduction (GP → TP) → regeneration (TP → RuBP).",
ans:["FIXATION: CO₂ from the air is added to RuBP (a 5-carbon acceptor) by the enzyme RuBisCO, forming an unstable 6C intermediate that immediately splits into TWO molecules of GP (glycerate-3-phosphate, 3C).","REDUCTION: GP is reduced to TP (triose phosphate, 3C) using hydrogen from NADPH (→ NADP⁺) and energy from ATP (→ ADP + Pi); this is where the electrons/energy from the light reactions are stored in organic form.","REGENERATION: most of the TP is used (with further ATP) to regenerate RuBP, so the cycle can continue fixing more CO₂; a small proportion of TP leaves the cycle to be made into glucose (two TP → one 6C glucose) and other organic molecules (amino acids, lipids).","Overall the cycle converts inorganic CO₂ into organic carbon using the ATP + NADPH made in the light-dependent stage; RuBP is recycled, so the cycle is self-sustaining as long as CO₂, ATP and NADPH are supplied."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-02",topics:["T5A"],ref:"WBI31 • Jan 2023 • Q3 style",title:"Limiting factors of photosynthesis",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"The rate of photosynthesis is measured at increasing light intensity at 25 °C and a fixed CO₂ concentration; the curve rises then plateaus.\n(a) Identify the limiting factor on the rising part and on the plateau. (2)\n(b) The same measurement is repeated at 35 °C (other conditions unchanged) and the plateau is HIGHER. Explain. (3)\n(c) At very high temperatures the rate crashes. Explain what happens to the enzymes. (2)",
hint:"Whichever increase raises the rate is limiting. Enzyme optima.",
ans:["(a) Rising part: LIGHT INTENSITY is limiting (increasing it raises the rate). Plateau: light is no longer limiting — CO₂ concentration (or the capacity of the Calvin-cycle enzymes at 25 °C) now limits the rate.","(b) At 35 °C the enzymes of the light-independent reactions (e.g. RuBisCO) work faster (more kinetic energy → more enzyme–substrate collisions) up to their optimum, so the maximum (saturated) rate is higher — the plateau shifts up before temperature starts to become limiting.","(c) Above the optimum (~35–40 °C for most plant enzymes) the heat breaks the weak bonds (H-bonds, ionic) holding the tertiary structure → enzymes DENATURE → active sites change shape → substrate no longer fits → the rate falls sharply (irreversibly if severe)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-03",topics:["T5A"],ref:"WBI31 • June 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Light-dependent reactions",marks:7,diff:3,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Describe what happens when light is absorbed by photosystem II, including the fate of the electrons and the source of the oxygen released. (4)\n(b) State the TWO products of the light-dependent reactions that are used in the Calvin cycle, and where they are used. (3)",
hint:"Photolysis of water → O₂. ATP + NADPH → reduction + regeneration.",
ans:["(a) Light energy excites electrons in the chlorophyll a reaction centre of PSII. The electrons are replaced by those from the PHOTOLYSIS of water (H₂O → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ + ½O₂) — so the O₂ released comes from WATER, not CO₂. The energised electrons pass down an electron transport chain, and the energy released is used to pump H⁺ across the thylakoid membrane; the H⁺ then drives ATP synthase (chemiosmosis) to make ATP (photophosphorylation). Finally the electrons re-energised by PSI reduce NADP⁺ (with H⁺) to NADPH.","(b) ATP and NADPH. NADPH provides the hydrogen/electrons to REDUCE GP to TP (and is oxidised back to NADP⁺); ATP provides the energy for both the reduction of GP and the REGENERATION of RuBP from TP (and is hydrolysed to ADP + Pi). (Oxygen is the by-product released to the atmosphere.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-04",topics:["T5A"],ref:"WBI31 • Oct 2022 • Q1 style",title:"Absorption & action spectra",marks:5,diff:1,time:"6 min",
q:"The absorption spectrum of a leaf extract shows peaks at ~430 nm and ~660 nm and a trough at ~550 nm.\n(a) Which colours of light are absorbed most and least, and why do leaves appear green? (3)\n(b) If an 'action spectrum' (photosynthetic rate vs wavelength) shows peaks at the same wavelengths, what does that indicate? (2)",
hint:"Blue + red absorbed; green reflected. Matching spectra = chlorophyll drives photosynthesis.",
ans:["(a) Blue-violet (~430 nm) and red (~660 nm) light are absorbed most strongly (the peaks); green/yellow (~550 nm) is absorbed least (the trough) — it is mostly REFLECTED/transmitted, which is why leaves appear green to our eyes.","(b) The action spectrum matching the absorption spectrum indicates that the SAME pigments that absorb the light (mainly chlorophyll a) are the ones driving photosynthesis — the wavelengths absorbed best are the wavelengths at which photosynthesis is fastest."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-05",topics:["T5B"],ref:"WBI31 • June 2021 • Q5 style",title:"Mark-release-recapture",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"A student estimates the number of woodlice in a 500 m² wood. First catch: 40 marked and released. Second catch: 50 woodlice, of which 10 are marked.\n(a) Calculate the estimated population size. (2)\n(b) State TWO assumptions of the method. (2)\n(c) If marked woodlice learn to avoid the traps, will the estimate be too high or too low? Explain. (3)",
hint:"N̂ = n×N/m. Marked must mix randomly and the population must be closed.",
ans:["(a) N̂ = (n × N) ÷ m = (40 × 50) ÷ 10 = 200 woodlice in the 500 m² wood.","(b) Any two: the marks are not lost and do not harm/affect catchability; marked individuals mix RANDOMLY with unmarked ones before the second catch; there is no net change in population size between catches (no births, deaths, immigration or emigration); all individuals are equally likely to be caught.","(c) TOO HIGH — if marked animals avoid the traps, fewer marked individuals are recaptured (m is smaller than it should be), and the formula N̂ = n×N/m divides by a too-small m, inflating the estimate (it interprets the low recapture fraction as a larger population)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-06",topics:["T5B"],ref:"WBI31 • Jan 2024 • Q6 style",title:"Statistics & error bars",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"Grassland quadrat samples of plant biomass (g/m²) are taken from two sites.\nSite A: mean 120, SD 30, n = 10. Site B: mean 150, SD 35, n = 10.\n(a) Calculate the 95% confidence interval for each site (use the t-value 2.26). (4)\n(b) The error bars (mean ± 95% CI) for the two sites OVERLAP. What can you conclude? (2)\n(c) State why the quadrats should be placed at random positions rather than 'where the grass looks typical'. (2)",
hint:"CI = mean ± t×SD/√n. Overlap → no significant difference. Random = unbiased sample.",
ans:["(a) Site A: 120 ± 2.26 × 30/√10 = 120 ± 2.26 × 9.49 = 120 ± 21.4 → 98.6 to 141.4 g/m². Site B: 150 ± 2.26 × 35/√10 = 150 ± 2.26 × 11.07 = 150 ± 25.0 → 125.0 to 175.0 g/m².","(b) The difference between the means MIGHT BE DUE TO CHANCE — we CANNOT conclude the two sites differ significantly (overlap means the true means could be equal); more samples or a formal test (e.g. t-test) would be needed. (Do NOT say 'no difference exists'.)","(c) Placing quadrats by eye BIASES the sample toward the chooser's expectation (e.g. choosing tussocks over bare patches) → the mean would not represent the whole site. Random placement (using random numbers) gives every part of the site an equal chance of being sampled, so the result is unbiased and statistically valid."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-07",topics:["T5B"],ref:"WBI31 • June 2022 • Q3 style",title:"Energy flow & the 10% rule",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"In a food chain, the producers fix 10 000 kJ/m²/yr of energy.\n(a) Estimate the energy available at the SECOND trophic level (primary consumers) and at the third (secondary consumers). (2)\n(b) Explain why only ~10% passes to the next level, naming THREE fates of the energy. (3)\n(c) Explain why food chains are usually short (3–5 links) and why top predators are rare. (1)",
hint:"×0.1 per step. Respiration/heat, uneaten parts, faeces. Pyramids of energy always upright.",
ans:["(a) Primary consumers: ~1 000 kJ/m²/yr. Secondary consumers: ~100 kJ/m²/yr.","(b) Most of the energy at each level is NOT passed on because: it is lost as HEAT through respiration (the largest fraction); much of the biomass is UNEATEN (roots, wood, bones, shells); and what is eaten includes indigestible material lost in FAECES (and energy used in urine/excretion). Only the energy assimilated into new biomass is available to the next level — roughly 10%.","(c) Because energy is lost at every step, after 4–5 transfers there is not enough energy left to support another full trophic level — top predators need large territories and few individuals (rare), and the chain cannot be long."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-08",topics:["T5B"],ref:"WBI31 • Oct 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Measuring primary productivity",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"In a pond, oxygen measurements over 24 h give: 12 cm³ O₂ produced in 12 h of light; 4 cm³ O₂ consumed in 12 h of darkness (per 1000 cm³ of water).\n(a) Calculate the gross primary production (GPP) and net primary production (NPP) in cm³ O₂ per 24 h. (3)\n(b) State why NPP, not GPP, is the energy available to herbivores. (2)\n(c) State ONE environmental factor that would limit NPP in a shaded pond. (1)",
hint:"GPP from the light period; NPP = GPP − respiration (the dark uptake).",
ans:["(a) GPP = O₂ made in the light = 12 cm³ per 12 h → 24 cm³ per 24 h. Respiration R = O₂ used in the dark = 4 cm³ per 12 h → 8 cm³ per 24 h. NPP = GPP − R = 24 − 8 = 16 cm³ O₂ per 24 h.","(b) GPP is the TOTAL energy fixed; a fraction is used by the producers' OWN respiration (R). NPP is what REMAINS as new biomass after that self-respiration — it is the mass/energy that can actually be eaten by herbivores.","(c) Light intensity (the shaded canopy limits the photosynthetically active radiation reaching the pond surface). (Also acceptable: CO₂ concentration, temperature, or nutrient availability such as phosphate/nitrate.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-09",topics:["T5C"],ref:"WBI31 • June 2023 • Q5 style",title:"The carbon cycle & human impacts",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Name the four main carbon reservoirs in the carbon cycle. (2)\n(b) State the natural flux that moves carbon from the atmosphere into the biosphere, and the one that returns it. (2)\n(c) Explain how EACH human activity — burning fossil fuels and deforestation — increases atmospheric CO₂. (3)",
hint:"Atmosphere, biosphere, oceans, rocks. Photosynthesis in, respiration/decomposition out.",
ans:["(a) Any four: the ATMOSPHERE (as CO₂), the BIOSPHERE (living organisms + soil organic matter), the OCEANS (dissolved CO₂ + carbonate shells/sediments), and SEDIMENTARY ROCKS/fossil fuels (long-term store).","(b) Into the biosphere: PHOTOSYNTHESIS (plants/algae fix atmospheric CO₂ into organic matter). Back to the atmosphere: RESPIRATION (by plants, animals and microbes) and DECOMPOSITION of dead matter (both release CO₂); combustion also returns it.","(c) Burning fossil fuels releases carbon that was removed from the cycle MILLIONS of years ago (stored in coal/oil/gas) as CO₂ on a timescale far faster than natural sinks can absorb it. Deforestation (i) removes the photosynthetic biomass that would otherwise be fixing CO₂, and (ii) the cleared/decomposed wood and peat respire/combust, releasing the stored carbon — both effects add net CO₂ to the atmosphere, tipping the natural balance (which previously had roughly equal in/out fluxes) toward a rising atmospheric concentration."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-10",topics:["T5C"],ref:"WBI31 • Jan 2024 • Q7 style",title:"Correlation vs causation — the warming debate",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"Ice-core and instrument records show atmospheric CO₂ and global mean temperature rising together since 1850.\nA sceptic argues: 'They correlate, but correlation does not prove that CO₂ causes the warming.'\n(a) State TWO independent lines of evidence that support CAUSATION rather than mere correlation. (4)\n(b) A climate model predicts +2.7 °C by 2100 under high emissions, with a range of +2.0 to +3.5 °C. State TWO reasons the prediction is a range rather than a single value. (4)",
hint:"Physics + isotopes + timing + models. Emissions assumptions + feedbacks + resolution.",
ans:["(a) Any two, developed: ① The PHYSICS is established — infrared absorption by CO₂ is measured directly in laboratories (the radiative forcing of CO₂ is a physical law, not an inferred pattern). ② ISOTOPIC FINGERPRINTING — the extra atmospheric carbon is enriched in ¹²C and lacks ¹⁴C, matching fossil-fuel (ancient, radiocarbon-dead) carbon rather than a natural reservoir such as the oceans. ③ TIMING — the CO₂ rise tracks the history of industrial fuel use, while solar irradiance has actually declined slightly since ~1980 even as temperatures rose, ruling out the Sun as the driver. ④ MODELLING — only climate models that include the added greenhouse-gas forcing reproduce the observed warming pattern; models without it do not.","(b) Any two, developed: ① Future EMISSIONS depend on human choices (energy policy, economics, population) that no model can predict — the 'high emissions' scenario is itself an assumption, and different scenarios give different warming. ② Key FEEDBACKS are imperfectly known (cloud cover, permafrost methane release, ice-albedo feedback, ocean carbon uptake) — they can amplify or dampen warming, so ensembles of models with different parameters give a spread of outcomes. (Also acceptable: coarse spatial RESOLUTION means small-scale processes are approximated, and the result is a probability-weighted range, not a point forecast.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI31-11",topics:["T5C"],ref:"WBI31 • Oct 2022 • Q6 style",title:"Climate models & their limits",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Describe how a general circulation model (GCM) works. (2)\n(b) State THREE limitations that mean GCM outputs are ranges, not precise forecasts. (4)",
hint:"Grid cells + physical equations. Emissions, feedbacks, resolution, local weather.",
ans:["(a) A GCM divides the atmosphere, oceans and land surface into a 3D grid of cells and solves the physical equations (fluid dynamics, thermodynamics, radiative transfer, cloud and surface processes) for each cell over time, with boundary conditions (e.g. greenhouse-gas concentrations from an emissions scenario) — running many such simulations (an ensemble) gives a distribution of outcomes.","(b) Any three, developed: ① Future emissions depend on human/policy choices that cannot be predicted (scenario uncertainty). ② Key feedbacks (clouds, permafrost methane, ice-albedo, carbon-cycle response) are imperfectly understood and must be approximated (parameterised). ③ Grid resolution is coarse (tens of km), so small-scale processes (convection, storm tracks, ice-sheet dynamics) are represented statistically, not directly. ④ GCMs predict GLOBAL/continental AVERAGES, not local weather or a specific town's rainfall — regional detail requires downscaling. (Hence outputs are given as ranges with confidence levels, used for planning rather than as precise predictions.)"]},
],
WBI32:[
{id:"BIO-WBI32-01",topics:["T6A"],ref:"WBI32 • June 2022 • Q2 style",title:"The bacterial growth curve",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"Bacteria are grown in a closed flask with a fixed nutrient supply. Describe the FOUR phases of the population curve, explaining the biology behind the shape of each phase. (8)",
hint:"Lag (adapting) → log (max division) → stationary (nutrients out) → death (toxins).",
ans:["LAG PHASE — numbers do not increase: cells are metabolically active, growing in size and synthesising the enzymes needed for the new medium, but not yet dividing (so the curve is flat).","LOG (EXPONENTIAL) PHASE — nutrients are plentiful and wastes low, so cells divide at a constant maximum rate by binary fission (N = N₀×2ⁿ); the curve rises steeply (exponentially) and the culture is most uniform.","STATIONARY PHASE — essential nutrients become depleted and toxic wastes (acids, alcohols) and low O₂ accumulate, so the growth rate falls to equal the death rate → net population constant (the curve plateaus); some cells form spores or stress proteins to survive.","DEATH (DECLINE) PHASE — severe nutrient exhaustion and high toxin levels make the death rate exceed the growth rate, so the population falls (the curve declines); only the hardiest cells (spores) persist."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-02",topics:["T6A"],ref:"WBI32 • Jan 2023 • Q3 style",title:"Counting bacteria — CFU",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"A student dilutes a broth 10⁻⁵ and plates 0.1 mL, counting 45 colonies after incubation.\n(a) Calculate the CFU/mL of the original broth. (2)\n(b) A haemocytometer count of the same original broth gives a HIGHER number than the plate count. Explain. (2)\n(c) State why a plate count is still the better measure of the number of bacteria that can cause infection. (2)\n(d) Name the instrument used to measure a culture's cloudiness, and what it must be calibrated against. (1)",
hint:"CFU = colonies ÷ volume × dilution factor. Viable vs total. OD vs standard curve.",
ans:["(a) CFU/mL = (colonies ÷ volume plated) × dilution factor = (45 ÷ 0.1) × 10⁵ = 450 × 10⁵ = 4.5 × 10⁷ CFU/mL.","(b) The haemocytometer counts ALL cells — live AND dead — in a known volume, whereas a plate count only counts cells that are VIABLE (able to divide and form a colony); the dead/injured fraction makes the microscopic total higher.","(c) Only LIVE bacteria can multiply and cause infection, so the viable count (CFU) is the biologically relevant number; dead cells are harmless (though they may still contain antigens/toxins).","(d) A colorimeter/spectrophotometer (measuring optical density/turbidity at a set wavelength), calibrated against a standard curve made from direct (plate) counts of the same culture."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-03",topics:["T6A"],ref:"WBI32 • June 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Aseptic technique",marks:6,diff:1,time:"7 min",
q:"A student is streaking a bacterium onto an agar plate.\n(a) Describe FIVE aspects of aseptic technique used during the procedure, explaining the purpose of at least two. (5)\n(b) Why must the inoculating loop be cooled before touching the culture? (1)",
hint:"Flame, lids, bench disinfectant, disinfector. Prevent contamination both ways.",
ans:["(a) Any five, each correct: work near the Bunsen flame (the rising hot air creates an updraft that sweeps airborne microbes away from the bench); FLAME-STERILISE the loop before and after (and cool it first); keep the plate lid on except momentarily to inoculate, and FLAME the neck of the bottle; don't talk over the open plate; disinfect the bench with ethanol before and after; label the plate; dispose of waste in the disinfector. Purposes (for two): flaming the loop kills any organisms on it so the culture is not contaminated and the worker is not exposed; keeping the lid closed prevents airborne spores/microbes falling into the agar.","(b) Because a red-hot loop would KILL the bacteria being inoculated (and damage the agar) — it must be cool enough to transfer live cells."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-04",topics:["T6A"],ref:"WBI32 • Jan 2024 • Q5 style",title:"Viral replication & why no antibiotics",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"(a) Describe the stages of the lytic replication cycle of a bacteriophage, in order. (5)\n(b) Explain why antibiotics have no effect on a viral infection. (3)",
hint:"Attach → enter → replicate → assemble → release. Antibiotics target bacterial structures.",
ans:["(a) 1. ATTACHMENT — the phage's tail fibres bind to specific receptor molecules on the bacterial cell surface (this determines host range). 2. ENTRY — the phage injects its DNA into the bacterium (the empty capsid stays outside). 3. REPLICATION/BIOSYNTHESIS — the bacterial machinery is hijacked: the phage DNA is copied and phage genes are transcribed/translated into new phage proteins (the host's own metabolism is diverted). 4. ASSEMBLY — new phage genomes and capsids are packaged into complete virions. 5. RELEASE — the bacterial cell Lyses (bursts), releasing the new phages to infect more cells.","(b) Antibiotics work by targeting structures or processes UNIQUE TO BACTERIA — e.g. the peptidoglycan cell wall, the 70S ribosome, or bacterial DNA/RNA enzymes. Viruses have NO cell wall, NO ribosomes and NO metabolism of their own — they merely use the host cell's machinery — so there is no bacterial target for the drug to attack (killing the host's machinery would harm the patient instead)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-05",topics:["T6A"],ref:"WBI32 • Oct 2023 • Q1 style",title:"Binary fission & growth maths",marks:5,diff:1,time:"6 min",
q:"A bacterium divides by binary fission every 20 minutes under ideal conditions.\n(a) Starting from a single cell, calculate the number of cells after 3 hours. (2)\n(b) If 5 × 10⁹ cells are present, how many divisions have occurred? (2)\n(c) State why the actual count in a flask would be LOWER than this calculation predicts after long growth. (1)",
hint:"N = N₀ × 2ⁿ. 180 min = 9 generations. Death phase limits it.",
ans:["(a) 3 h = 180 min = 9 divisions of 20 min → N = 1 × 2⁹ = 512 cells.","(b) 2ⁿ = 5 × 10⁹ → n = log₂(5 × 10⁹) ≈ 32.2 → about 32 divisions (2³² ≈ 4.3 × 10⁹; 2³³ ≈ 8.6 × 10⁹).","(c) Because the flask is a CLOSED system — nutrients deplete and wastes accumulate, so cells enter stationary/death phases and the net population stops growing (and eventually falls) rather than doubling indefinitely."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-06",topics:["T6B"],ref:"WBI32 • June 2021 • Q3 style",title:"Innate defences & inflammation",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Name TWO physical and TWO chemical barriers that prevent pathogen entry. (4)\n(b) Describe the local inflammatory response at a cut, stating the purpose of EACH change. (3)",
hint:"Skin, mucus/cilia, acid, lysozyme. Vasodilation, permeability, chemotaxis.",
ans:["(a) Physical (any two): the skin (a continuous, keratinised, acidic, dry barrier); mucous membranes that trap pathogens in mucus; cilia in the respiratory tract that sweep mucus (and trapped microbes) up and out; stomach acid (pH ~1–2) killing ingested microbes. Chemical (any two): lysozyme in tears/saliva (breaks bacterial cell walls); defensins/antimicrobial peptides in saliva and on skin; sebum and the acidic pH of the skin; the acidic vaginal pH.","(b) The response: ① VASODILATION of local blood vessels → increased blood flow → redness and warmth (and brings more immune cells). ② INCREASED CAPILLARY PERMEABILITY → fluid and plasma proteins leak into the tissue → swelling (oedema) that dilutes the pathogen/toxin and brings opsonins. ③ Release of chemical mediators (e.g. histamine from mast cells) that cause PAIN (deters further injury) and CHEMOTAXIS — they guide more phagocytes (neutrophils) to the site to engulf and digest the pathogens. (Overall purpose: isolate the pathogen and recruit the phagocytes that destroy it.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-07",topics:["T6B"],ref:"WBI32 • Jan 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Antibodies, B cells & T cells",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"(a) Describe the humoral response: what a B cell does when activated, and what the plasma cells and memory cells produce/do. (4)\n(b) Describe the role of cytotoxic T cells in cell-mediated immunity. (2)\n(c) State THREE ways antibodies help to destroy a pathogen. (2)",
hint:"B → plasma (antibodies) + memory. CD8 kills infected cells. Neutralise, agglutinate, opsonise.",
ans:["(a) When a B cell's receptor binds its specific antigen (usually with help from a helper T cell that has presented the antigen), it undergoes CLONAL EXPANSION — dividing rapidly into many identical B cells. Most differentiate into PLASMA CELLS, which secrete large quantities of ANTIBODIES specific to that antigen into the blood/lymph; the rest become MEMORY B CELLS, which persist for years and mount a faster, stronger response on re-exposure.","(b) Cytotoxic (CD8⁺) T cells recognise antigens presented on the surface of INFECTED (or cancerous) host cells (on MHC I), bind to them, and induce APOPTOSIS (programmed cell death) of the target cell — destroying the pathogen's factory from the inside.","(c) Any two: NEUTRALISATION — antibodies bind to and block the pathogen's toxins or its attachment sites (preventing infection of cells); AGGLUTINATION — antibodies cross-link many pathogens together into clumps that are easier for phagocytes to engulf; OPSONISATION — antibodies coat the pathogen's surface, marking it so phagocytes recognise and engulf it more readily (also activates the complement system)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-08",topics:["T6B"],ref:"WBI32 • June 2022 • Q5 style",title:"Vaccination & the memory response",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Explain why a secondary immune response is faster and stronger than a primary response. (3)\n(b) Distinguish ACTIVE from PASSIVE immunity, giving one example of each. (2)\n(c) A vaccine contains a harmless part of a pathogen (a subunit). Explain why it protects but does not cause the disease. (2)",
hint:"Memory cells already present. Own antibodies vs given antibodies. Antigen without the weapon.",
ans:["(a) The primary response leaves behind MEMORY B and T cells (long-lived). On re-exposure to the same antigen, these memory cells are already present and specific — they undergo clonal expansion within HOURS (not days), producing far MORE antibodies (mostly IgG) faster → the pathogen is cleared before it can cause disease.","(b) ACTIVE immunity — the body's OWN immune system makes the antibodies and memory cells (after natural infection or VACCINATION); it is LONG-LASTING (memory cells). PASSIVE immunity — ready-made ANTIBODIES are transferred in (e.g. maternal antibodies across the placenta/in breast milk, or an injected antiserum/immunoglobulin); it is SHORT-LIVED (weeks–months) and creates NO memory cells.","(c) The subunit (e.g. a surface protein or toxoid) is an ANTIGEN — it is recognised by B cells and triggers the full adaptive response (antibodies + memory cells) — but it LACKS the pathogen's 'weapons' (it cannot replicate or produce the damaging virulence factors), so it cannot establish an infection or cause the disease."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-09",topics:["T6B"],ref:"WBI32 • Jan 2024 • Q6 style",title:"How antibiotics work",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Explain how a penicillin (a β-lactam antibiotic) kills a bacterium. (3)\n(b) In a disc-diffusion test, an antibiotic disc produces a large zone of inhibition around it on a bacterial lawn. Explain what the zone shows, and why a LARGER zone means a more effective drug. (4)",
hint:"Block cell-wall cross-linking → lysis. Zone = where growth was prevented.",
ans:["(a) Penicillin binds to and inhibits the transpeptidase (penicillin-binding protein) that CROSSLINKS the peptidoglycan strands of the bacterial cell wall. New walls are then weak/incomplete; because the cytoplasm is under high (turgor) pressure in a hypotonic environment, water enters and the weakened wall cannot hold the pressure → the cell swells and LYZES. (It only affects bacteria — human cells have no peptidoglycan wall.)","(b) The antibiotic diffuses from the disc into the agar, its concentration falling with distance from the disc. Where the concentration exceeds the bacterium's minimum inhibitory concentration, growth is blocked — producing a clear ZONE of no bacteria around the disc. A LARGER zone means the drug inhibited growth at greater distances (i.e. at lower concentrations) → the bacterium is more SENSITIVE to that drug (a more effective choice); a small or absent zone indicates resistance/ineffectiveness."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-10",topics:["T6B"],ref:"WBI32 • June 2023 • Q7 style",title:"Antibiotic resistance — a scenario",marks:7,diff:3,time:"9 min",
q:"A patient takes a β-lactam antibiotic for a bacterial infection. After 3 days of a 10-day course they feel better and stop. Two years later the same type of infection no longer responds to that antibiotic.\n(a) Explain how the incomplete course allowed resistance to become dominant. (4)\n(b) State TWO ways the doctor could have reduced the risk of this outcome. (3)",
hint:"The drug selects — it doesn't create. Survivors reproduce. Stewardship + combinations.",
ans:["(a) The antibiotic does not CREATE resistance — it SELECTS for it. The original population contained a few resistant individuals (by mutation or an acquired resistance gene, e.g. a β-lactamase gene on a plasmid). The drug killed the sensitive majority, removing their competition; the resistant survivors then reproduced, and because the course was STOPPED early (leaving even more survivors and a smaller sensitive pool), the resistant strain became the dominant population. Two years later the infection is caused by that resistant strain, so the same β-lactam no longer works.","(b) Any two: ① complete the FULL prescribed course (clearing the sensitive population so selection pressure on the resistant minority is removed); ② confirm the diagnosis and the strain's SENSITIVITIES first (culture + disc diffusion) and prescribe only the drug the bacterium is sensitive to (antibiotic stewardship — not a broad-spectrum 'just in case'); ③ use COMBINATION therapy (several drugs at once, as in TB) so a single resistance mutation is unlikely to confer resistance to all of them; ④ use the correct dose for the correct duration."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-11",topics:["T6C"],ref:"WBI32 • Jan 2023 • Q2 style",title:"PCR",marks:8,diff:2,time:"9 min",
q:"(a) Describe the THREE steps of one PCR cycle, in order, giving an approximate temperature for each and what happens. (6)\n(b) State the special property of the DNA polymerase used, and the organism it comes from. (1)\n(c) After 30 cycles, roughly how many copies of the target region are present (starting from one)? (1)",
hint:"Denature 95 → anneal ~55 → extend 72. Taq (T. aquaticus). 2ⁿ.",
ans:["(a) 1. DENATURATION (~94–95 °C) — the high heat breaks the hydrogen bonds, separating the double-stranded DNA template into single strands. 2. ANNEALING (~50–65 °C) — the temperature is lowered so the short PRIMER sequences (complementary to the ends of the target region) bind (anneal) to their complementary positions on the single-stranded templates. 3. EXTENSION (~72 °C) — the DNA polymerase extends each primer, adding free nucleotides to synthesise a new complementary strand of the target region. Each cycle doubles the target (exponential amplification).","(b) It must be HEAT-STABLE (it survives the repeated 95 °C denaturation steps without being destroyed) — it is Taq polymerase, from the thermophilic bacterium Thermus aquaticus.","(c) 2³⁰ ≈ 1 × 10⁹ (about a billion) copies."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI32-12",topics:["T6C"],ref:"WBI32 • June 2022 • Q6 style",title:"DNA profiling — exclusion",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"A crime-scene sample and a suspect's sample are profiled at 4 STR loci.\nLocus 1: scene 12,14 / suspect 12,14. Locus 2: scene 18 / suspect 18,20. Locus 3: scene 9,11 / suspect 9,11. Locus 4: scene 7,7 / suspect 7,9.\n(a) Is the suspect a possible source of the scene DNA? (1)\n(b) Work through each locus to justify your answer. (4)\n(c) Explain why a DNA 'match' is reported as a probability rather than as absolute proof. (3)",
hint:"One inconsistent locus excludes. Match probability = population frequencies + error.",
ans:["(a) NO — the suspect is EXCLUDED as the source.","(b) Locus 1: scene 12,14 vs suspect 12,14 — consistent (the scene's alleles are both present in the suspect). Locus 2: scene 18 (single band) vs suspect 18,20 — consistent (the scene's 18 is present in the suspect). Locus 3: scene 9,11 vs suspect 9,11 — consistent. Locus 4: scene 7,7 (homozygous) vs suspect 7,9 — INCONSISTENT: a 7,9 individual can pass only a 7 or a 9, so they could never produce a 7,7 sample; the scene's two 7s cannot come from this suspect. A single inconsistent locus is sufficient to EXCLUDE the suspect.","(c) Because a profile is a set of allele SIZES, and the 'match probability' is calculated from POPULATION ALLELE FREQUENCIES — it is the statistical chance that a RANDOM, unrelated person would share the profile (e.g. 1 in a billion), not a certainty. In addition, lab error, contamination or a mixed sample can produce an apparent match, and close relatives share many alleles — so a match must be weighed with the rest of the evidence, not treated as infallible."]},
],
WBI41:[
{id:"BIO-WBI41-01",topics:["T7A"],ref:"WBI41 • June 2022 • Q2 style",title:"Aerobic respiration — the stages",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"(a) For EACH stage of aerobic respiration — glycolysis, the link reaction, the Krebs cycle, the electron transport chain — state its LOCATION in the cell and its main products (ATP, NADH/FADH₂, CO₂). (6)\n(b) State the total ATP yield per glucose (modern estimate). (1)\n(c) State which stage releases ALL the CO₂, and where the O₂ 'goes'. (1)",
hint:"Cytoplasm / matrix / matrix / inner membrane. ~30–32. O₂ → water at the end of the ETC.",
ans:["(a) GLYCOLYSIS — cytoplasm (cytosol): glucose → 2 pyruvate; net 2 ATP + 2 NADH (no CO₂, no O₂ used). LINK REACTION — mitochondrial matrix: 2 pyruvate → 2 acetyl-CoA; releases 2 CO₂ + 2 NADH. KREBS CYCLE — mitochondrial matrix: per glucose (2 turns) 4 CO₂ + 6 NADH + 2 FADH₂ + 2 ATP (substrate-level). ELECTRON TRANSPORT CHAIN — inner mitochondrial membrane (cristae): the 10 NADH + 2 FADH₂ feed electrons; O₂ is the final acceptor (→ water); ~26–28 ATP made by chemiosmosis (ATP synthase).","(b) ~30–32 ATP per glucose (the older '36–38' figure overestimates the cost of shuttling cytoplasmic NADH).","(c) The CO₂ comes from the LINK reaction (2) and the KREBS cycle (4) — not glycolysis; the O₂ is reduced at the END of the electron transport chain, combining with H⁺ and electrons to form WATER (the water in the overall equation)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-02",topics:["T7A"],ref:"WBI41 • Jan 2023 • Q3 style",title:"Respiratory quotient",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"A respirometer shows a sample of germinating seeds producing 120 cm³ CO₂ and consuming 150 cm³ O₂ in one hour.\n(a) Calculate the RQ. (1)\n(b) Deduce what the seeds are respiring. (2)\n(c) After the seeds are given a sugar solution, the RQ is measured as 1.0. Explain the change. (2)\n(d) State the RQ values for carbohydrate, fat and protein. (2)",
hint:"RQ = CO₂/O₂. 0.8 = mixed (fat present). Sugar → carbs → 1.0.",
ans:["(a) RQ = CO₂ produced ÷ O₂ consumed = 120 ÷ 150 = 0.80.","(b) An RQ of 0.80 is below 1.0 (carbohydrate) and close to the fat value (~0.7), so the seeds are respiring a MIX of substrates with a substantial FAT component (germinating seeds store both starch and oil; the sub-unity RQ shows significant lipid oxidation).","(c) Adding a sugar solution supplies an abundant, easily-mobilised CARBOHYDRATE, which the seeds now preferentially respire; carbohydrate has an RQ of 1.0 (6 CO₂ produced per 6 O₂ used), so as carbohydrate use dominates the measured RQ rises to 1.0.","(d) Carbohydrate = 1.0; fat ≈ 0.7 (fats are more reduced → need more O₂ → release less CO₂ per O₂); protein ≈ 0.9."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-03",topics:["T7A"],ref:"WBI41 • June 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Why oxygen is essential",marks:7,diff:3,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Describe how the electron transport chain and chemiosmosis produce most of the ATP in aerobic respiration. (4)\n(b) Explain what would happen to ATP production if oxygen were removed from the system. (3)",
hint:"ETC pumps H⁺; gradient drives ATP synthase. O₂ = final acceptor; remove it and the chain backs up.",
ans:["(a) NADH and FADH₂ (from glycolysis, the link reaction and the Krebs cycle) donate their high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain — a series of protein complexes and mobile carriers in the INNER MITOCHONDRIAL MEMBRANE. As electrons pass down the chain, their energy is used to pump H⁺ from the matrix into the intermembrane space, building an electrochemical proton gradient. OXYGEN, the final electron acceptor, combines with the electrons and H⁺ to form water. The H⁺ then flows back into the matrix through ATP synthase, and the energy of this flow (chemiosmosis) phosphorylates ADP to ATP — producing ~26–28 of the ~30–32 ATP per glucose.","(b) Without O₂ there is no FINAL electron acceptor, so the electron transport chain backs up (electrons have nowhere to go); proton pumping stops, the H⁺ gradient collapses, and ATP synthase can no longer make ATP by chemiosmosis. Aerobic ATP production falls to just the 2 (glycolysis) + 2 (Krebs) substrate-level ATPs, and the cell must switch to ANAEROBIC respiration to regenerate the NAD⁺ that glycolysis needs."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-04",topics:["T7A"],ref:"WBI41 • Oct 2022 • Q5 style",title:"Anaerobic respiration & the oxygen debt",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) In a yeast culture sealed without oxygen, glucose is converted to ethanol and CO₂. Name the TWO stages after glycolysis and the enzyme of the first. (3)\n(b) Explain why the NADH produced in glycolysis MUST be re-oxidised, even though anaerobic respiration yields no extra ATP. (3)",
hint:"Pyruvate → ethanal (pyruvate decarboxylase) → ethanol (alcohol dehydrogenase). NAD⁺ is glycolysis's cofactor.",
ans:["(a) Stage 1: pyruvate is DECARBOXylated to ethanal (acetaldehyde) + CO₂ by the enzyme PYRUVATE DECARBOXYLASE. Stage 2: ethanal is REDUCED to ethanol by ALCOHOL DEHYDROGENASE (using the NADH from glycolysis).","(b) Glycolysis requires NAD⁺ as an electron acceptor (in the step that produces NADH); in the absence of O₂ the electron transport chain cannot re-oxidise NADH back to NAD⁺. If NAD⁺ were not regenerated, glycolysis — the cell's ONLY source of ATP in the absence of O₂ — would stop after a few seconds. Reducing ethanal to ethanol (or pyruvate to lactate in animals) therefore exists to RE-OXIDISE NADH to NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue; the ethanol/lactate are by-products of that recycling, not additional energy sources (hence no extra ATP is made)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-05",topics:["T7B"],ref:"WBI41 • June 2022 • Q3 style",title:"The sliding-filament mechanism",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"Describe, in order, the events from the arrival of a nerve impulse to a sarcomere's shortening, naming the role of Ca²⁺, tropomyosin, the myosin head and ATP. (8)",
hint:"Impulse → T-tubule → SR releases Ca²⁺ → troponin → tropomyosin moves → cross-bridge → power stroke → ATP detaches/re-energises.",
ans:["1. The nerve impulse (action potential) travels along the sarcolemma and down the T-TUBULES into the muscle fibre. 2. This triggers the SARCOPLASMIC RETICULUM to release Ca²⁺ into the sarcoplasm. 3. Ca²⁺ binds to TROPONIN on the thin (actin) filaments, causing a shape change that pulls TROPOMYOSIN aside, EXPOSING the myosin-binding sites on actin. 4. The MYOSIN HEADS (energised with bound ATP/ADP+Pi) bind to the exposed sites, forming CROSS-BRIDGES. 5. The myosin head PIVOTS — the POWER STROKE — pulling the thin filament towards the centre of the sarcomere (releasing ADP + Pi); the sarcomere shortens (I-band and H-band narrow, A-band unchanged). 6. A fresh ATP BINDS to the myosin head, DETACHING it from actin. 7. ATP is hydrolysed (→ ADP + Pi), RE-ENERGISING the head for the next cycle. 8. Relaxation occurs when Ca²⁺ is actively pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (Ca²⁺-ATPase), tropomyosin re-covers the binding sites, and the cross-bridge cycle stops. (Note ATP is needed both to detach the head and to re-energise it — and to pump Ca²⁺ back; when ATP runs out after death, the heads stay bound → rigor mortis.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-06",topics:["T7B"],ref:"WBI41 • Jan 2024 • Q4 style",title:"Homeostasis — controlling heart rate",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) Define homeostasis and negative feedback. (2)\n(b) During exercise, explain the negative-feedback loop that raises heart rate: name the receptor/stimulus, the control centre and the effector. (3)\n(c) State the difference between the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions' effect on heart rate. (2)",
hint:"Stable internal environment. Reverses the deviation. Chemoreceptors, medulla, heart (sympathetic ↑ / vagal ↓).",
ans:["(a) Homeostasis is the maintenance of a STABLE INTERNAL ENVIRONMENT (e.g. temperature, pH, blood glucose, CO₂) despite external changes. Negative feedback is a control mechanism in which a RECEPTOR detects a deviation from a set point, a CONTROL CENTRE compares and decides, and an EFFECTOR makes a corrective change that REVERSES the deviation, returning the system to the set point (it stabilises rather than amplifies).","(b) Stimulus/receptor: the working muscles respire more → blood CO₂ rises (and O₂ falls) → CHEMORECEPTORS (in the carotid/aortic bodies and the medulla) detect the ↑CO₂/↓pH (and the ↑core temperature is sensed by thermoreceptors; baroreceptors sense the BP change). Control centre: the MEDULLA OBLONGATA (cardioacceleratory centre) + the hypothalamus. Effector: the SYMPATHETIC nerves to the heart (and adrenaline from the adrenal medulla) increase the SAN's firing rate → heart rate (and stroke volume) rises → cardiac output rises → O₂ delivery and CO₂ removal improve → the stimulus is corrected (CO₂/pH return toward normal).","(c) The SYMPATHETIC division INCREASES heart rate (noradrenaline/adrenaline on the SAN — 'fight or flight'); the PARASYMPATHETIC division (the vagus nerve, acetylcholine) DECREASES heart rate ('rest and digest'). They act antagonistically, with parasympathetic dominance at rest and sympathetic dominance during exercise/stress."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-07",topics:["T7B"],ref:"WBI41 • June 2023 • Q5 style",title:"Ventilation & the exercise response",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"During a 20-minute run a student's breathing rate rises from 12 to 30 breaths/min and tidal volume from 0.5 to 1.2 dm³.\n(a) Calculate the minute ventilation at rest and during the run. (2)\n(b) Explain, in terms of homeostasis, why the rate and depth increased. (3)\n(c) Ventilation stays elevated for several minutes after the run. Explain why. (2)",
hint:"Rate × tidal volume. ↑CO₂ → chemoreceptors → medulla → respiratory muscles. EPOC repays the debt.",
ans:["(a) Resting: 12 × 0.5 = 6 dm³/min. During the run: 30 × 1.2 = 36 dm³/min (a six-fold increase).","(b) The working muscles respire more, producing more CO₂ (and using O₂). Blood CO₂ rises → more H⁺ (CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻) → pH falls. CHEMORECEPTORS detect the ↑CO₂/↓pH and signal the medullary respiratory centres, which increase BOTH the rate and the depth (tidal volume) of breathing (effectors = the diaphragm and intercostal muscles). This raises ventilation, blowing off CO₂ and taking in O₂, returning blood CO₂/pH toward the set point — a negative-feedback loop.","(c) To repay the 'oxygen debt' (excess post-exercise oxygen consumption): the extra O₂ is used to clear/convert lactate, replenish muscle and liver glycogen, restore the blood's O₂ stores and normalise the elevated hormones/temperature — so ventilation (and heart rate) remain above resting until these are complete, then fall back as CO₂/pH normalise and the feedback loop resets."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-08",topics:["T7B"],ref:"WBI41 • Oct 2023 • Q1 style",title:"Muscle fibre types",marks:5,diff:1,time:"6 min",
q:"Compare SLOW (Type I) and FAST (Type II) muscle fibres with respect to: mitochondria, myoglobin/colour, main respiration type, fatigue resistance and a typical use. (5)",
hint:"Red endurance vs white power. Aerobic vs anaerobic.",
ans:["Slow (Type I, 'red'): MANY mitochondria; rich in myoglobin (red); relies mainly on AEROBIC respiration; FATIGUE-RESISTANT (sustained); used for endurance/steady work (marathon running, posture, long swimming).","Fast (Type II, 'white'): FEWER mitochondria; less myoglobin (whiter); relies on ANAEROBIC (glycolytic) respiration; FATIGUES quickly but produces more FORCE/power; used for sprints, jumping and bursts of power.","(A full comparison table earns the marks — one row per feature; noting that Type II subdivides into IIa/IIx and that training can shift the balance is a bonus.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-09",topics:["T7C"],ref:"WBI41 • June 2022 • Q4 style",title:"Blood glucose homeostasis",marks:8,diff:2,time:"10 min",
q:"After a large meal, blood glucose rises to 9 mmol/L and then falls back to about 5 mmol/L within two hours.\n(a) Name the hormone responsible, its source and the cells that detect the change. (3)\n(b) State THREE ways the hormone lowers the blood glucose. (3)\n(c) Name the hormone that acts when blood glucose falls, its source and its main action. (2)",
hint:"Insulin from the beta cells (detected by the pancreas + hypothalamus). Glucagon from the alpha cells.",
ans:["(a) INSULIN, secreted by the β-cells of the pancreas (islets of Langerhans); the rise is detected by the β-cells themselves (and by osmoreceptors/receptors in the hypothalamus).","(b) Any three: increases glucose UPTAKE into liver and muscle cells (upregulating glucose transporters, e.g. GLUT4); promotes GLYCOGENESIS (glucose → glycogen in liver and muscle); promotes conversion of glucose to FAT (lipogenesis); inhibits glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis (so less glucose is released). Each removes glucose from the blood, returning it toward the ~5 mmol/L set point (negative feedback).","(c) GLUCAGON, from the α-cells of the pancreas; its main action on the liver is to promote GLYCOGENOLYSIS (liver glycogen → glucose released into the blood) and GLUCONEOGENESIS (glucose made from amino acids/glycerol), raising the blood glucose. (The two hormones are antagonistic.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-10",topics:["T7C"],ref:"WBI41 • Jan 2023 • Q5 style",title:"ADH & osmoregulation",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"A person drinks very little water on a hot day; their blood becomes more concentrated (lower water potential).\n(a) Trace the negative-feedback response from the stimulus to the urine produced: name the receptor, the control centre, the hormone, the gland, the target and the effect on the urine. (6)\n(b) Explain why the urine produced is more concentrated, in terms of the collecting duct and the medulla. (2)",
hint:"Osmoreceptors → hypothalamus → ADH (posterior pituitary) → collecting-duct aquaporins → less, darker urine.",
ans:["(a) Stimulus: blood water potential FALLS (becomes more concentrated). Receptor: OSMORECEPTORS in the hypothalamus detect it. Control centre: the hypothalamus. Hormone + gland: it triggers the POSTERIOR PITUITARY to release ADH (vasopressin) into the blood. Target: the cells of the COLLECTING DUCTS of the kidney (they insert more AQUAPORIN water channels into their membranes). Effect: MORE water is reabsorbed from the filtrate back into the (hypertonic) medullary blood → the urine volume FALLS and becomes MORE concentrated (darker), diluting the blood back toward the set point (negative feedback).","(b) The loop of Henle maintains a HYPERTONIC medulla (high solute concentration). With ADH present, the collecting ducts become permeable to water, so water leaves the duct by OSMOSIS down the water-potential gradient into the concentrated medullary interstitium/blood — leaving a small volume of urine with a high solute (urea/NaCl) concentration. (Without ADH the ducts are impermeable and a large volume of dilute urine is produced.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-11",topics:["T7C"],ref:"WBI41 • June 2023 • Q6 style",title:"The nephron — filtration & reabsorption",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"(a) Contrast GLomerular FILTRATION with selective REABSORPTION: where each occurs, what drives it, and what passes/is kept. (4)\n(b) An uncontrolled diabetic has blood glucose of 14 mmol/L and glucose in their urine (glycosuria). Explain, in terms of the proximal convoluted tubule. (4)",
hint:"Pressure at the glomerulus vs active transport in the tubules. Transport maximum (Tm) saturation.",
ans:["(a) FILTRATION occurs at the GLOMERULUS (inside Bowman's capsule): it is driven by BLOOD PRESSURE (the afferent arteriole is wider than the efferent, maintaining high pressure) and is NON-SELECTIVE for small molecules — water, ions, glucose, amino acids and urea pass into the filtrate, while the large plasma proteins and blood cells are too big to cross the filtration barrier. REABSORPTION occurs in the TUBULES (mostly the proximal convoluted tubule): it is SELECTIVE and driven by ACTIVE TRANSPORT (e.g. Na⁺/glucose co-transport, powered by Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase — hence the PCT cells' many mitochondria) plus OSMOSIS for water; ALL glucose and amino acids (up to the transport maximum) and most water/ions are kept, while urea and excess solutes are left in the filtrate. (The DCT/collecting duct then fine-tune Na⁺/water under aldosterone/ADH.)","(b) The PCT normally reabsorbs ALL filtered glucose via Na⁺-glucose co-transporters (SGLT), but these carriers have a TRANSPORT MAXIMUM (Tm) — a saturation point. At a blood glucose of 14 mmol/L the filtered load EXCEEDS the Tm, so the carriers are saturated and cannot reabsorb the excess; the surplus glucose remains in the filtrate and appears in the urine (glycosuria). The glucose then draws water out of the tubule by osmosis (osmotic diuresis) → large volumes of dilute urine (polyuria) and thirst."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI41-12",topics:["T7C"],ref:"WBI41 • Oct 2022 • Q7 style",title:"Thermoregulation on a hot day",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"On a hot day a person's core temperature rises above the ~37 °C set point.\n(a) State the receptor, the control centre and the effector response that cools the body. (3)\n(b) Explain how EACH of vasodilation and sweating removes heat. (3)\n(c) State what is meant by hyperthermia/heatstroke and why it is dangerous. (1)",
hint:"Thermoreceptors → hypothalamus → vasodilation + sweating. Evaporation removes latent heat.",
ans:["(a) Receptor: THERMORECEPTORS (in the skin and in the hypothalamus) detect the rise. Control centre: the HYPOTHALAMUS (compares to the ~37 °C set point). Effectors: the smooth muscle in skin blood vessels (VASEODILATION) and the SWEAT GLANDS (sweating) — plus reduced metabolic heat production (no shivering, lower metabolism).","(b) VASODILATION widens the skin's blood vessels, bringing MORE WARM BLOOD to the body surface, where heat is lost to the cooler surroundings by radiation and convection (the skin also flushes red). SWEATING secretes sweat (mostly water + a little salt) onto the skin; the EVAPORATION of that sweat removes LATENT HEAT from the skin (evaporation is an endothermic process), cooling the blood flowing beneath — the drier the air, the faster the evaporation and the greater the cooling.","(c) Heatstroke (hyperthermia) is a failure of cooling in which the core temperature rises above ~40 °C (e.g. in hot, humid air where sweat cannot evaporate); it is dangerous because the enzymes and structures of the body denature/damage at such temperatures — leading to organ failure and death if not rapidly cooled."]},
],
WBI42:[
{id:"BIO-WBI42-01",topics:["T8A"],ref:"WBI42 • June 2022 • Q3 style",title:"The action potential",marks:9,diff:3,time:"11 min",
q:"A neurone's membrane potential is recorded as: −70 mV (rest) → −55 mV → +40 mV → −70 mV → −85 mV → −70 mV.\n(a) Name each phase in order and state the ion movement (or pump) responsible for each. (6)\n(b) Explain why a stimulus during the +40 mV peak cannot fire a second action potential. (2)\n(c) State what 'all-or-none' means for action potentials. (1)",
hint:"Rest → threshold → depol (Na⁺ in) → peak → repol (K⁺ out) → hyperpol → pump restores. Inactivation gates.",
ans:["(a) −70 mV = RESTING potential (maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump + K⁺ leak channels). −55 mV = THRESHOLD reached (voltage-gated Na⁺ channels start to open). +40 mV = PEAK of DEPOLARISATION (voltage-gated Na⁺ channels fully open, Na⁺ rushes IN down its electrochemical gradient). Back to −70 mV = REPOLARISATION (Na⁺ channels inactivate; voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, K⁺ flows OUT). −85 mV = HYPERPOLARISATION/undershoot (K⁺ channels close slowly, so K⁺ leaves a little too long, overshooting the resting level). Return to −70 mV = the Na⁺/K⁺ PUMP actively restores the original ion distribution (3 Na⁺ out : 2 K⁺ in per ATP).","(b) At the peak the voltage-gated Na⁺ channels have their INACTIVATION gates closed (they are in the inactivated state). No matter how strong a new stimulus is, those channels cannot re-open until they recover from inactivation, so no new depolarisation can occur — this is the ABSOLUTE REFRACTORY PERIOD (it also guarantees one-way conduction).","(c) 'All-or-none' means that once threshold is reached, the action potential fires at a FIXED (maximum) amplitude; a stronger stimulus does not make a bigger action potential — it can only make it fire MORE OFTEN (frequency coding)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-02",topics:["T8A"],ref:"WBI42 • Jan 2023 • Q4 style",title:"Neurotransmission at a synapse",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"Describe, in order, what happens at a cholinergic synapse (e.g. the neuromuscular junction) from the arrival of an action potential to the stopping of the signal, naming the role of Ca²⁺, acetylcholine and acetylcholinesterase. (8)",
hint:"AP → Ca²⁺ in → vesicle fusion → ACh diffuses → receptors open → Na⁺ in → AChE breaks ACh down.",
ans:["1. An action potential arrives at the axon terminal (presynaptic end). 2. It depolarises the terminal, opening VOLTAGE-GATED Ca²⁺ CHANNELS; Ca²⁺ floods in. 3. The Ca²⁺ triggers the SYNAPTIC VESICLES (containing acetylcholine, ACh) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane and RELEASE their ACh into the synaptic cleft by exocytosis. 4. ACh DIFFUSES across the cleft and BINDS to specific receptor proteins (nicotinic ACh receptors — ligand-gated Na⁺ channels) on the postsynaptic membrane. 5. The receptors open, Na⁺ flows in, and the postsynaptic membrane DEPOLARISES (an excitatory postsynaptic potential); if threshold is reached, an action potential fires in the postsynaptic cell (the muscle fibre contracts). 6. The signal STOPS because the enzyme ACETYLCHOLINESTERASE (AChE) in the cleft rapidly BREAKS DOWN the ACh (into choline + acetate), so it can no longer bind the receptors; the choline is taken back up into the presynaptic terminal to re-make ACh. (The rapid breakdown is what prevents continuous stimulation.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-03",topics:["T8A"],ref:"WBI42 • June 2023 • Q5 style",title:"Myelination & saltatory conduction",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) Describe how saltatory conduction works in a myelinated axon. (3)\n(b) In multiple sclerosis the myelin is destroyed. Explain the two main consequences for that axon. (2)\n(c) State how a local anaesthetic (e.g. lidocaine) blocks a nerve impulse. (1)",
hint:"Jump node to node. Slower + higher energy cost. Block Na⁺ channels.",
ans:["(a) Myelin is an electrical INSULATOR (high resistance, low capacitance), and the voltage-gated Na⁺/K⁺ channels are concentrated at the NODES OF RANVIER (the gaps). An action potential is generated at one node, and the resulting local current flows internally under the myelin (with little charge loss) to the NEXT node, depolarising it to threshold, where a new action potential fires — the impulse effectively JUMPS from node to node (saltatory conduction), which is much faster (up to ~120 m/s) and far less energy-costly (only the nodes need pumping) than continuous conduction.","(b) Without myelin the saltatory jump is lost, so the impulse must propagate CONTINUOUSLY along the whole membrane: ① conduction becomes MUCH SLOWER (and can be blocked altogether if the depolarisation fails to reach the next segment), and ② the energy cost rises sharply because the Na⁺/K⁺ pump must now restore the gradients across the entire length, not just the nodes — causing the weakness, sensory loss and fatigue of MS.","(c) It BLOCKS the voltage-gated Na⁺ CHANNELS, so the membrane cannot depolarise → no action potential is generated/conducted → the impulse (e.g. a pain signal) never reaches the brain (the nerve is numb)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-04",topics:["T8A"],ref:"WBI42 • Oct 2022 • Q6 style",title:"Drugs at the synapse",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"Three substances act at a cholinergic synapse:\n(i) curare, (ii) a nerve agent that inhibits acetylcholinesterase, (iii) a reuptake blocker.\nFor EACH, state where it acts and the effect on transmission. (6)",
hint:"Receptor block / enzyme block / reuptake block. Paralysis vs continuous stimulation vs enhanced signal.",
ans:["(i) CURARE acts as a (competitive) ANTAGONIST at the postsynaptic ACh RECEPTOR — it blocks ACh from binding, so no Na⁺ enters and no depolarisation occurs → transmission is BLOCKED → flaccid paralysis (no muscle contraction).","(ii) AChE INHIBITION (nerve agents) acts in the CLEFT — the enzyme that normally breaks down ACh is blocked, so ACh BUILDS UP and keeps binding the receptors → continuous stimulation of the postsynaptic cell (initial spasticity, then desensitisation/failure — the mechanism of nerve-gas poisoning).","(iii) A REUPTAKE BLOCKER acts on the presynaptic reuptake mechanism — it stops the neurotransmitter being cleared from the cleft by reuptake, so its concentration stays HIGH for longer → the postsynaptic signal is ENHANCED/prolonged (the principle behind some antidepressants that block serotonin/noradrenaline reuptake)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-05",topics:["T8B"],ref:"WBI42 • Jan 2024 • Q2 style",title:"Brain regions & damage",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) State the main function of EACH brain region: cerebrum (cerebral cortex), cerebellum, medulla oblongata, hypothalamus. (4)\n(b) State the likely consequence of damage to the cerebellum. (1)\n(c) State why a person with a severed spinal cord below the chest loses BOTH sensation and movement in the legs. (2)",
hint:"Higher functions / coordination / vital autonomic / homeostasis + pituitary. Ataxia. Sensory up + motor down tracts.",
ans:["(a) CEREBRUM (cerebral cortex) — the seat of higher functions: conscious thought, memory, learning, language, planning voluntary movement, and processing the senses (sensory and motor areas). CEREBELLUM — coordinates VOLUNTARY movement, balance and posture (fine-tunes the motor cortex's output). MEDULLA OBLONGATA — the vital AUTOMATIC centre: controls heartbeat, breathing rate and blood pressure, and the reflexes of vomiting, swallowing, coughing and sneezing (directly continuous with the spinal cord). HYPOTHALAMUS — the neuro-endocrine LINK and homeostatic control centre: controls body temperature, hunger/thirst, circadian rhythms, and drives the pituitary gland (e.g. ADH release).","(b) Loss of COORDINATION — ataxia: uncoordinated, jerky movements, tremor, difficulty with balance and fine motor tasks (strength is preserved, but the movements are poorly timed).","(c) The spinal cord carries BOTH the ASCENDING sensory tracts (information from the legs up to the brain) and the DESCENDING motor tracts (commands from the brain down to the leg muscles). A complete severance cuts both, so no sensory information from the legs reaches the brain (no feeling) and no motor commands reach the leg muscles (no voluntary movement) below the injury."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-06",topics:["T8B"],ref:"WBI42 • June 2022 • Q4 style",title:"The reflex arc",marks:7,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"(a) List the FIVE components of a reflex arc, in order. (3)\n(b) Explain why a reflex response is faster than a conscious (brain-mediated) response. (2)\n(c) In the knee-jerk reflex, the quadriceps contracts while the hamstring relaxes. Name this and state its purpose. (2)",
hint:"Receptor → sensory → integration (relay) → motor → effector. Fewer synapses + spinal level. Reciprocal inhibition.",
ans:["(a) In order: ① RECEPTOR (detects the stimulus and transduces it into an electrical signal) → ② SENSORY (afferent) NEURONE (carries the impulse to the CNS) → ③ INTEGRATION CENTRE in the spinal cord (usually via one RELAY/interneurone; in a monosynaptic reflex the sensory neurone synapses directly on the motor neurone) → ④ MOTOR (efferent) NEURONE (carries the impulse to the effector) → ⑤ EFFECTOR (the muscle contracts or the gland secretes).","(b) The pathway is SHORT: it has FEWER SYNAPSES (each synapse adds ~0.5 ms of delay) and it is completed AT THE SPINAL CORD without the signal travelling up to the brain and back down — so the response occurs before the brain is even aware of the stimulus (the brain is informed afterwards).","(c) This is RECIPROCAL INHIBITION — an inhibitory interneuron simultaneously RELAXES the antagonist (hamstring) while the agonist (quadriceps) contracts, so the two muscles do not fight each other and the movement is smooth and efficient."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-07",topics:["T8B"],ref:"WBI42 • Jan 2023 • Q5 style",title:"Phototropism & auxin",marks:8,diff:3,time:"10 min",
q:"A seedling's shoot tip is intact and light enters from the left.\n(a) Explain, in terms of auxin, why the shoot bends toward the light. (4)\n(b) A mica barrier (which blocks auxin transport) is inserted on the RIGHT side of the coleoptile; light still enters from the left. Predict the result and explain. (3)\n(c) The shoot tip is removed from a third seedling. Will it show phototropism? Explain. (1)",
hint:"Tip senses light → auxin to shaded side → shaded side grows more. Mica blocks the transport. No tip = no sensor/source.",
ans:["(a) Light is detected at the shoot TIP. In response, auxin is transported LATERALLY down the SHADED (right) side, so the shaded side has a HIGHER auxin concentration than the lit side. Because auxin PROMOTES cell elongation in stems, the shaded-side cells elongate MORE than the lit-side cells → the shoot BENDS toward the light (the faster-growing shaded side pushes the stem over toward the light source).","(b) The tip still detects the left-sided light and tries to move auxin to the shaded (right) side, but the MICA barrier blocks the auxin transport down that side, so auxin cannot accumulate on the right. Without the higher auxin concentration on the shaded side, that side does not elongate more → the normal bending toward the light is PREVENTED (the shoot grows straight or bends abnormally). This shows the bending is caused by auxin TRANSPORT, not by light directly stimulating growth on the lit side.","(c) NO — the shoot tip is both the light SENSOR and the auxin SOURCE; with it removed there is no photoreceptor to detect the directional light and no auxin to be redistributed, so no lateral gradient forms and no phototropic bending occurs."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-08",topics:["T8B"],ref:"WBI42 • Oct 2023 • Q3 style",title:"Sympathetic vs parasympathetic",marks:6,diff:1,time:"7 min",
q:"Complete the comparison: for EACH organ — heart, pupil, gut (digestion) — state the SYMPATHETIC effect and the PARASYMPATHETIC effect. (6)",
hint:"Fight-or-flight vs rest-and-digest. Up vs down on each.",
ans:["HEART: sympathetic → INCREASES heart rate and force of contraction (noradrenaline/adrenaline on the SAN); parasympathetic (vagus, acetylcholine) → DECREASES heart rate.","PUPIL: sympathetic → DILATES the pupil (radial muscle contracts — more light in, 'alert'); parasympathetic → CONSTRICTS the pupil (circular muscle contracts).","GUT/DIGESTION: sympathetic → INHIBITS (slows) gut movement and secretion (diverts blood and energy to muscle — 'fight or flight'); parasympathetic → STIMULATES gut peristalsis and the secretion of digestive juices ('rest and digest').","(The two divisions usually act ANTAGONISTICALLY on the same organ, providing a balanced, reversible control of the internal environment.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-09",topics:["T8C"],ref:"WBI42 • June 2023 • Q7 style",title:"Cloning the insulin gene",marks:9,diff:3,time:"11 min",
q:"Outline, in order, the steps from the human insulin gene to purified insulin produced by E. coli, naming the key enzyme/tool at each step and explaining why a bacterium can make a human protein. (9)",
hint:"Isolate (cDNA via reverse transcriptase) → cut (same restriction enzyme) → ligase → transform → select (antibiotic) → express (universal code) → harvest.",
ans:["1. ISOLATE the human insulin gene — from human DNA, or (better, since bacteria cannot splice introns) make cDNA from insulin mRNA using REVERSE TRANSCRIPTASE. 2. CUT both the insulin gene and a plasmid VECTOR with the SAME RESTRICTION ENZYME (e.g. EcoRI) so they have complementary (matching) sticky ends. 3. JOIN the insert and the vector with DNA LIGASE → a RECOMBINANT PLASMID (insulin gene + the plasmid's antibiotic-resistance marker gene). 4. TRANSFORM the recombinant plasmid into E. coli (heat-shock or electroporation to make the cells competent); only a small fraction take it up. 5. SELECT the transformed cells by growing them on the matching ANTIBIOTIC (e.g. ampicillin) — only cells with the plasmid survive. 6. EXPRESS: grow the selected bacteria in a fermenter — the UNIVERSAL GENETIC CODE means the bacteria's own RNA polymerase and ribosomes read the human gene correctly, so they transcribe and translate it to produce HUMAN INSULIN. 7. HARVEST & PURIFY the insulin from the culture for use as medicine (identical to the body's own insulin, made at industrial scale — cheaper and purer than the old animal-pancreas extract).","(A full answer names each step's tool — reverse transcriptase, restriction enzyme, ligase, antibiotic marker, fermenter — and explicitly states the universal code as the reason cross-species expression works.)"]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-10",topics:["T8C"],ref:"WBI42 • Jan 2024 • Q3 style",title:"Restriction enzymes & ligase",marks:6,diff:2,time:"7 min",
q:"(a) State what a restriction enzyme does and why cutting the gene AND the vector with the SAME enzyme is essential. (3)\n(b) State what DNA ligase does. (1)\n(c) Explain how a plasmid's antibiotic-resistance gene allows selection of transformed cells. (2)",
hint:"Cuts at specific sequences → matching sticky ends. Phosphodiester bonds. Only resistant cells survive the antibiotic.",
ans:["(a) A restriction enzyme (endonuclease) CLEAVES DNA at a specific short recognition sequence (e.g. EcoRI recognises GAATTC), producing either sticky (overhanging) or blunt ends. Cutting BOTH the target gene and the vector with the SAME enzyme produces COMPLEMENTARY sticky ends, so the two fragments can base-pair with each other — if different enzymes with incompatible ends were used, the fragments could not join correctly.","(b) DNA ligase joins the DNA fragments by forming the PHOSPHODIESTER BOND between the sugar-phosphate backbones, making the recombinant DNA molecule stable and permanent.","(c) The plasmid carries a GENE for resistance to a specific antibiotic (e.g. ampicillin). After transformation, the cells are grown on media containing that antibiotic: only the cells that took up the plasmid (and therefore express the resistance gene) SURVIVE, while non-transformed cells die — so the colonies that grow are the transformed ones (the marker 'selects' them)."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-11",topics:["T8C"],ref:"WBI42 • June 2022 • Q8 style",title:"GMOs & gene editing — the ethics",marks:6,diff:2,time:"8 min",
q:"Discuss the ETHICAL and SOCIAL issues of: (i) genetically modified (GM) food crops, and (ii) editing the human GERMLINE with CRISPR. Give a reasoned conclusion. (6)",
hint:"Benefits (yields, nutrition, cures) vs risks (ecology, health, corporate control, consent for future generations). Support with regulation; oppose premature germline editing.",
ans:["(i) GM CROPS — For: higher yields and drought/pest resistance help feed a growing population; nutritional enhancement (e.g. golden rice's β-carotene) can combat deficiency diseases; Bt crops reduce the need for sprayed insecticides. Against: ecological risk (gene flow to wild relatives creating 'superweeds', possible harm to non-target organisms such as pollinators, reduced biodiversity from monocultures); long-term health effects of novel proteins are not fully known (the precautionary principle); and corporate control — a few companies patent the genes, raising dependency, 'terminator-seed' concerns and inequity between rich and poor nations. (ii) HUMAN GERMLINE EDITING — For (in principle): the potential to eliminate severe monogenic diseases (e.g. some forms of cystic fibrosis, Huntington's) from a family line. Against: it changes EVERY future generation without their consent; the technology is not yet safe (off-target edits, mosaicism); it risks 'designer babies' and widening inequality; the 2018 'CRISPR babies' case was widely condemned and the researcher convicted. REASONED CONCLUSION: clear medical benefits (somatic gene therapy, well-tested GM crops) should be supported WITH strict safety testing, regulation, transparent labelling and public debate; but premature HERITABLE human editing should be opposed until safety, consent and equity are settled — the consensus is that germline editing for reproduction is not yet safe or ethical."]},
{id:"BIO-WBI42-12",topics:["T8C"],ref:"WBI42 • Oct 2023 • Q4 style",title:"PCR, gene therapy & gene editing",marks:5,diff:2,time:"6 min",
q:"(a) State the difference between PCR, gene therapy and CRISPR gene editing. (3)\n(b) A CRISPR treatment edits a patient's OWN stem cells to treat sickle-cell disease. Explain why this is 'somatic' and not 'germline', and why that distinction matters ethically. (2)",
hint:"Amplify vs deliver a working gene vs cut/edit in place. Own cells, not inherited.",
ans:["(a) PCR (polymerase chain reaction) AMPLIFIES a specific DNA region (making millions of copies using primers + Taq polymerase) — it copies, it does not change the organism. GENE THERAPY delivers a FUNCTIONAL COPY of a gene (usually via a viral vector) into a patient's cells to correct/replace a faulty one — it ADDS a working gene. CRISPR gene EDITING uses a guide RNA + the Cas9 nuclease to CUT the genome at a precise, chosen sequence, so the cell's own repair machinery can fix, insert or DELETE that specific sequence — it EDITS the gene in place (more precise than simply adding a copy).","(b) It is 'somatic' because it edits the patient's OWN body (stem) cells, which are NOT passed to children — the change affects only that individual. The distinction matters because germline editing (sperm/egg/embryo) WOULD be inherited by all future generations, raising consent, safety and 'designer baby' issues that somatic (non-heritable) therapy does not — which is why somatic gene therapy is broadly supported while germline editing is not yet considered acceptable."]},
]
};
